{"abstract":"Background: Acute pulmonary embolism (PE) induces ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxia and increases pulmonary pressure and right ventricular (RV) afterload, entailing potentially fatal RV failure within a short timeframe. Cardiopulmonary factors may respond differently to increased clot burden. We aimed to elucidate immediate cardiopulmonary responses during successive PE episodes in a porcine model. Methods: This was a randomized, controlled, blinded study of repeated measurements. Twelve pigs were randomly assigned to receive sham procedures or consecutive PEs every 15 min until doubling of mean pulmonary pressure. Cardiopulmonary assessments were conducted at 1, 2, 5, and 13 min after each PE using pressure-volume loops, invasive pressures, and arterial and mixed venous blood gas analyses. ANOVA and mixed-model statistical analyses were applied. Results: Pulmonary pressures increased after the initial PE administration (p &amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;amp;#x003C; 0.0001), with a higher pulmonary pressure change compared to pressure change observed after the following PEs. Conversely, RV arterial elastance and pulmonary vascular resistance was not increased after the first PE, but after three PEs an increase was observed (p = 0.0103 and p = 0.0015, respectively). RV dilatation occurred following initial PEs, while RV ejection fraction declined after the third PE (p = 0.004). RV coupling exhibited a decreasing trend from the first PE (p = 0.095), despite increased mechanical work (p = 0.003). Ventilatory variables displayed more incremental changes with successive PEs. Conclusion: In an experimental model of consecutive PE, RV afterload elevation and dysfunction manifested after the third PE, in contrast to pulmonary pressure that increased after the first PE. Ventilatory variables exhibited a more direct association with clot burden.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lyhne, Mads Dam","Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Kramer, Anders","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-737216"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Lyhne, Mads Dam"],"keyword":["Animal model","Animals","Blood Gas Analysis","Disease Models Animal","Female","Gas exchange","Male","Pulmonary Embolism physiopathology","Pulmonary circulation","Random Allocation","Right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular function","Swine","Vascular Resistance physiology","Ventilation perfusion mismatch","Ventricular Dysfunction Right physiopathology","Ventricular Function Right physiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"immediate cardiopulmonary responses to consecutive pulmonary embolism a randomized controlled experimental study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12890-024-03006-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-737216","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11093735","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38745282","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85193206931","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-6bcd9f25-921e-43f2-9925-075f4a35c964"],"keyword":["Animal model","Animals","Blood Gas Analysis","Disease Models, Animal","Female","Gas exchange","Male","Pulmonary Embolism/physiopathology","Pulmonary circulation","Random Allocation","Right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular function","Swine","Vascular Resistance/physiology","Ventilation-perfusion mismatch","Ventricular Dysfunction, Right/physiopathology","Ventricular Function, Right/physiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FCA02C44864ED9619C7DF122DBB06158"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12890-024-03006-9","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Anaesthesiology and Operation, Intensive care"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Dam","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Gammelgaard","key":"schultzpau","last":"Schultz","locid":"9825d55c-97ee-4bab-a003-801dcd6d6df8","name":"Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"kramerpau","last":"Kramer","locid":"be3123b5-5f55-4c8a-930c-f0f39cbc2981","name":"Kramer, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-5546-8693","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38745282"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12890-024-03006-9","issn":["14712466"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMC Pulmonary Medicine","paperid":"233","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Pulmonary Medicine","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85193206931"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-6bcd9f25-921e-43f2-9925-075f4a35c964"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Immediate cardiopulmonary responses to consecutive pulmonary embolism : a randomized, controlled, experimental study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-737216","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Soil-steel composite bridges (SSCB) are extensively utilized for constructing culverts and small-span bridges in numerous countries worldwide, particularly in Europe and North America. The widespread adoption of SSCBs is attributed to their various advantages, such as swift construction, easy installation, low maintenance, straightforward renovations or extensions, and high geometric flexibility, enabling seamless adaptation to terrain conditions and functional requirements. An alternative solution for such structures is the use of reinforced concrete bridges (RCB), including concrete boxes, slabs, frames, or beams bridges. Under the global shift towards a low-carbon strategy and reduction of greenhouse emissions from the transport sector, assessing potential solutions for culverts or small bridges, it is essential to consider their environmental impact. And this is a practice increasingly common across various economic sectors, including engineering. This paper aims to compare the environmental impact of SSCBs and RCBs by analyzing selected 29 equivalent case studies from several European countries using the cradle-to-gate Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology. Two key environmental impact indicators are selected for evaluation: global warming potential and energy consumption. Data for the analysis of these indicators are derived from Environmental Product Declarations (EPD) and the environmental database Ecoinvent v.3.8. The case studies demonstrate the advantages of soil-steel bridges over reinforced concrete bridges in the chosen environmental indicators. The main reason for the superior performance of SSCBs lies in their highly efficient use of material mass to ensure the same functionality and load capacity comparing to the concrete solutions. The main load-bearing component of SSCBs is a thin and lightweight corrugated steel plate, which acts as a flexible shell interacting effectively with the surrounding compacted soil. This solution significantly reduces overall resource consumption and carbon emissions.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Du, Guangli","Czerepak, Adam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-742876"],"event":{"end":"2024-06-28","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2024-06-24","title":"12th IABMAS International Conference on Bridge Maintenance, Safety and Management","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Poland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","ViaCon Group"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"low carbon solution for short span bridges soil steel composite bridges versus concrete bridges","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003483755-154","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-742876","identifier":[{"id":"85200366343","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-67ade775-a40e-42ad-92d4-0c5db04d3388"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7751A5A0711FA556BD0D880A36AEDAC4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003483755-154","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"rem","url":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/oa-edit/10.1201/9781003483755/bridge-maintenance-safety-management-digitalization-sustainability-jens-sandager-jensen-dan-frangopol-jacob-wittrup-schmidt?context=ubx&refId=4b47a0fd-5d2d-428e-9371-9e7a1e27565e","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Den