{"abstract":"This case report describes laryngeal oedema occurring in a 35-year-old woman with chronic bowel-associated dermatosis-arthritis syndrome, and stenosis of the left main bronchus. The oedema was attributed to persistent cough exacerbated by delayed treatment and intubation-related irritation. Evaluations ruled out inflammatory, autoimmune, and malignant causes. Literature lacks on specific descriptions of cough-induced laryngeal oedema, emphasizing the need for a multidisciplinary approach and early intervention in complex cases to prevent severe hospitalizations in patients with known serious conditions and symptom exacerbation.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Nielsen, Martine S.","Borup, Maria Bisgaard","Kjeldsen, Jens","Stenger, Michael","Madsen, Anders Rørbæk","Schmidt, Henrik","Ellingsen, Torkell","Davidsen, Jesper Rømhild"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-782975"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Martine S."],"keyword":["Adult","Cough etiology","Female","Humans","Intubation Intratracheal adverse effects","Respiration Artificial adverse effects"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hosteinduceret respiratorkrævende larynxødem","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.61409/V12230792","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-782975","identifier":[{"id":"85197724780","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38953687","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-3a0575f7-ecec-42ce-8446-f180b3e8b543"],"keyword":["Adult","Cough/etiology","Female","Humans","Intubation, Intratracheal/adverse effects","Laryngeal Edema/etiology","Respiration, Artificial/adverse effects"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-55603C18DCD1064A8BCD922625944783"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/275077110/V12230792_WEB.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.61409/V12230792","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Odense Respiratory Research Unit (ODIN)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care (Kolding)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martine Siw","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"a37263da-160f-410f-84ee-d565008e7134","name":"Nielsen, Martine S.","name_org":"Nielsen, Martine Siw","orcid":"0000-0002-5999-3372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria Bisgaard","key":"boruppau","last":"Borup","locid":"2279e795-daa2-4b2e-8310-9876fb1698b2","name":"Borup, Maria Bisgaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"kjeldsenpau","last":"Kjeldsen","locid":"4893b496-ebc1-4498-92aa-836500b4cb45","name":"Kjeldsen, Jens","orcid":"0000-0001-8148-6572","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"stengerpau","last":"Stenger","locid":"736bd795-eb91-41ee-ad81-fdf944151b2d","name":"Stenger, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3605-1785","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"rørbækmadsenpau","last":"Rørbæk Madsen","locid":"686fd288-67ef-4a05-9e17-01d4cef47b86","name":"Madsen, Anders Rørbæk","name_org":"Rørbæk Madsen, Anders","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"9789920f-4b31-4f28-8211-97afced9d61e","name":"Schmidt, Henrik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Torkell Juulsgaard","key":"ellingsenpau","last":"Ellingsen","locid":"12189359-9c0f-4bf1-8b75-be844c75b85b","name":"Ellingsen, Torkell","name_org":"Ellingsen, Torkell Juulsgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-0426-4962","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Rømhild","key":"davidsenpau","last":"Davidsen","locid":"bfd91489-501f-49d3-aa3b-d9e8ea9a77f0","name":"Davidsen, Jesper Rømhild","orcid":"0000-0003-4128-4014","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38953687"],"pub":{"doi":"10.61409/v12230792","issn":["00415782","13994174"],"issn_org":["00415782"],"issue":"20","journal":"Ugeskrift for Læger","paperid":"V12230792","publisher":"Lægeforeningen","status":"p","title":"Ugeskrift for Læger","volume":"186"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85197724780"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3a0575f7-ecec-42ce-8446-f180b3e8b543"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Hosteinduceret respiratorkrævende larynxødem","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-782975","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Angioedema is a rare but potentially life-threatening adverse drug reaction in patients receiving angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEis). Research suggests that susceptibility to ACEi-induced angioedema (ACEi-AE) involves both genetic and nongenetic risk factors. Genome- and exome-wide studies of ACEi-AE have identified the first genetic risk loci. However, understanding of the underlying pathophysiology remains limited. Objective: We sought to identify further genetic factors of ACEi-AE to eventually gain a deeper understanding of its pathophysiology. Methods: By combining data from 8 cohorts, a genome-wide association study meta-analysis was performed in more than 1000 European patients with ACEi-AE. Secondary bioinformatic analyses were conducted to fine-map associated loci, identify relevant genes and pathways, and assess the genetic overlap between ACEi-AE and other traits. Finally, an exploratory cross-ancestry analysis was performed to assess shared genetic factors in European and African-American patients with ACEi-AE. Results: Three genome-wide significant risk loci were identified. One of these, located on chromosome 20q11.22, has not been implicated previously in ACEi-AE. Integrative secondary analyses highlighted previously reported genes (BDKRB2 [bradykinin receptor B2] and F5 [coagulation factor 5]) as well as biologically plausible novel candidate genes (PROCR [protein C receptor] and EDEM2 [endoplasmic reticulum degradation enhancing alpha-mannosidase like protein 2]). Lead variants at the risk loci were found with similar effect sizes and directions in an African-American cohort. Conclusions: The present results contributed to a deeper understanding of the pathophysiology of ACEi-AE by (1) providing further evidence for the involvement of bradykinin signaling and coagulation pathways and (2) suggesting, for the first time, the involvement of the fibrinolysis pathway in this adverse drug reaction. An exploratory cross-ancestry comparison implicated the relevance of the associated risk loci across diverse ancestries.","author_count":51,"contributors":["Mathey, Carina M.","Maj, Carlo","Eriksson, Niclas","Krebs, Kristi","Westmeier, Julia","David, Friederike S.","Koromina, Maria","Scheer, Annika B.","Szabo, Nora","Wedi, Bettina","Wieczorek, Dorothea","Amann, Philipp M.","Löffler, Harald","Koch, Lukas","Schöffl, Clemens","Dickel, Heinrich","Ganjuur, Nomun","Hornung, Thorsten","Buhl, Timo","Greve, Jens","Wurpts, Gerda","Aygören-Pürsün, Emel","Steffens, Michael","Herms, Stefan","Heilmann-Heimbach, Stefanie","Hoffmann, Per","Schmidt, Börge","Mavarani, Laven","Andresen, Trine","Sørensen, Signe Bek","Andersen, Vibeke","Vogel, Ulla","Landén, Mikael","Bulik, Cynthia M.","Bygum, Anette","Magnusson, Patrik K. E.","von Buchwald, Christian","Hallberg, Pär","Ostrowski, Sisse Rye","Sørensen, Erik","Pedersen, Ole Birger","Ullum, Henrik","Erikstrup, Christian","Bundgaard, Henning","Milani, Lili","Rasmussen, Eva Rye","Wadelius, Mia","Ghouse, Jonas","Sachs, Bernhardt","Nöthen, Markus M.","Forstner, Andreas J."],"cuid":["S04-091.21-767798"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corpau":["DBDS Genomic Consortium","Danish Blood Donor Study Genomic Consortium","Estonian Biobank Research Team"],"corresponding_author":["Forstner, Andreas J."],"country":["Austria","Estonia","Germany","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Danube Private University","Danube Private University (DPU)","Essen University Hospital","Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices","Forschungszentrum Jülich GmbH","Goethe University Frankfurt","Hannover Medical School","Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai","Karolinska Institute","Karolinska Institutet","Medical University of Graz","Philipps University of Marburg","RWTH Aachen University","Research Center Jülich","Ruhr University Bochum","SLK Hospital Heilbronn","Sahlgrenska University Hospital","Ulm University","Ulm University Medical Center","University Hospital Bonn","University Hospital Essen","University Hospital RWTH Aachen","University Hospital Ulm","University Hospital of Bonn","University Hospital of Essen","University Medical Center Göttingen","University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf","University of Bonn","University of Duisburg-Essen","University of Gothenburg","University of Göttingen","University of Marburg","University of North Carolina","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill","University of Tartu","Uppsala University","Uppsala University and Uppsala Clinical Research Center"],"keyword":["Angioedema","Angioedema chemically induced","Angiotensin Converting Enzyme Inhibitors adverse effects","Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor","Bradykinin","Drug Related Side Effects and Adverse Reactions","Genome Wide Association Study","Humans","Meta analysis","angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor induced angioedema"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI","DI-GV-WORK"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["SLK-Kliniken Heilbronn"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-GV-WORK","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"meta analysis of ace inhibitor induced angioedema identifies novel risk locus","src":["au","ku","nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2023.11.921","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was funded by in-house resources of both the Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) and the Institute of Human Genetics, University Hospital of Bonn (grant nos. V-17454/68502/2017-2020 and V2021.5/68502/2022-2024). The research was supported by the Swedish Research Council, Swedish Heart and Lung Foundation, and Clinical Research Support Avtal om Läkarutbildning och Forskning at Uppsala University. The Swedish Twin Registry at Karolinska Institutet that provided controls was supported by and received funding from the Swedish Research Council. The SNP&SEQ Technology Platform at Uppsala University that performed part of the genotyping is supported by the Swedish Research Council. Computations were in part done on resources provided by the Uppsala Multidisciplinary Center for Advanced Computational Science. Swedegene receives funding from the Swedish Research Council. Data analyses were carried out in part in the High-Performance Computing Center of the University of Tartu. The Anorexia Nervosa Genetics Initiative is an initiative of the Klarman Family Foundation. Funding support for the VanMar cohorts was provided by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) grants R01-HL079184 and U01-HL065962, and the RIKEN Institute. L.M. and K.K. are supported by funding from Estonian Research Council grants (grant no. PRG184). M.K. is supported by Baszucki Brain Research Fund, Milken Institute Center for Strategic Philanthropy. J.G. is supported by BRIDGE—Translational Excellence Programme (NNF20SA0064340). T.A. is supported by the Region of Southern Denmark. M.L. received funding support from the Klarman Foundation. The Estonian Biobank Research Team has contributed to data creation and QC. The samples and associated genotype and phenotype data of the VanMar cohorts used in this study were provided by Vanderbilt University Medical Center (dbGaP accession phs000438.v1.p1). This research has been conducted using the UKB Resource (application no. 60928). This work was funded by in-house resources of both the Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) and the Institute of Human Genetics, University Hospital of Bonn (grant nos. V-17454/68502/2017-2020 and V2021.5/68502/2022-2024). The research was supported by the Swedish Research Council , Swedish Heart and Lung Foundation, and Clinical Research Support Avtal om Läkarutbildning och Forskning at Uppsala University . The Swedish Twin Registry at Karolinska Institutet that provided controls was supported by and received funding from the Swedish Research Council . The SNP&SEQ Technology Platform at Uppsala University that performed part of the genotyping is supported by the Swedish Research Council . Computations were in part done on resources provided by the Uppsala Multidisciplinary Center for Advanced Computational Science. Swedegene receives funding from the Swedish Research Council. Data analyses were carried out in part in the High-Performance Computing Center of the University of Tartu. The Anorexia Nervosa Genetics Initiative is an initiative of the Klarman Family Foundation . Funding support for the VanMar cohorts was provided by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) grants R01-HL079184 and U01-HL065962, and the RIKEN Institute. L.M. and K.K. are supported by funding from Estonian Research Council grants (grant no. PRG184). M.K. is supported by Baszucki Brain Research Fund, Milken Institute Center for Strategic Philanthropy. J.G. is supported by BRIDGE—Translational Excellence Programme (NNF20SA0064340). T.A. is supported by the Region of Southern Denmark. M.L. received funding support from the Klarman Foundation."],"id":"S04-091.21-767798","identifier":[{"id":"38300190","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85186255164","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-b541ba10-d828-4066-a572-44606a355a1c","ku-9d1945fe-0b09-4195-93f8-cd839304b846","nfa-1ed6a668-cf82-4f8c-8fa8-6a85a426195c","sdu-3711c9b5-23ce-412e-ba33-e4a257373b36"],"keyword":["Angioedema/chemically induced","Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors/adverse effects","Bradykinin","Drug-Related Side Effects and Adverse Reac