2-s2.0-85199371306"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-37228afb-50f8-4f9e-9521-9aa481c4e7b6"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Impact of gender in patients with device-related thrombosis after left atrial appendage closure – A sub-analysis from the multicenter EUROC-DRT-registry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-752745","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: Emotional distress in partners to brain injury survivors are related to or result from the life changing brain injury. These consequences may be underpinned and maintained by shame, self-criticism, disenfranchised grief alongside an inability to self-soothe. Compassion Focused Therapy (CFT) was developed to address shame and self-criticism and foster the ability to self-soothe. The concepts and clinical application of CFT have yet to be explored in depth with partners to brain injury survivors. Therefore, we developed BackUp© which is a six-session manual-based short term psychological intervention for relatives to brain injury survivors. It combines elements from Compassion Focused Therapy, Cognitive Behavioral Therapy, Mindfulness and Narrative perspectives adjusted to relatives. Methods: This study is a small feasibility study of BackUp© given in an online format. Five relatives of brain injury survivors will receive an online psychological intervention according to the BackUp© programme (6 sessions). Self-report measures of quality of life (WHO5), Self-compassion (Neff-SCS) and Cohen's Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) will be collected pre- and post-intervention, analyzed and compared with the general population. Moreover, an evaluating interview will be conducted with all five participants post-intervention. Results: At the present the study is ongoing, but data collection will be completed in June and preliminary results will be presented at the conference and discussed in the light of current neurorehabilitation and the need for psychosocial interventions for relatives.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Thøgersen, Cecilie Marie Schmidt","Glintborg, Chalotte","Bak Sommer, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-757072"],"event":{"end":"2024-08-30","place":"Trondheim, no","start":"2024-08-27","title":"15th Nordic Meeting in Neuropsychology","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-OTH","DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"backup a manual based psychotherapeutic intervention for relatives to adults with acquired brain injuries","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/768141282/BackUp_190824.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-757072","ids":["aau-9b711e81-4d8e-49ea-af3f-c43712699df9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CB776DE33DC3C60DBB6EFA8CF5BAD4FB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/768141282/BackUp_190824.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Cognition, Development and Clinical Psychology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other hospitals","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-OTH","l3nl":"Other Hospitals","l3nn":"Other Hospitals","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Hospitals","namevar":["Vejlefjord Rehabilitation"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cecilie Marie Schmidt","key":"thøgersenpau","last":"Thøgersen","locid":"158714cb-d21d-4b63-b15e-54297872691b","name":"Thøgersen, Cecilie Marie Schmidt","orcid":"0000-0002-2960-6335","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Chalotte","key":"glintborgpau","last":"Glintborg","locid":"6fcac4a6-64c9-48df-8832-83a287f5865f","name":"Glintborg, Chalotte","orcid":"0000-0001-6074-7219","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"baksommerpau","last":"Bak Sommer","name":"Bak Sommer, Jens","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Hospitals","Universities","vejlefjord rehabilitation"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-9b711e81-4d8e-49ea-af3f-c43712699df9"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"BackUp© A manual based psychotherapeutic intervention for relatives to adults with acquired brain injuries.","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-757072","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background The association between social and built environments plays a crucial role in influencing physical activity levels. However, a thorough understanding of their combined impact remains unclear. This scoping review seeks to clarify the interplay between social environments and opportunities for physical activity within different built environments, with a particular focus on the implications of socioeconomic status and urban planning on physical activity participation. Methods We conducted a systematic literature search across several databases to identify studies exploring the associations between social factors, built environment characteristics, and physical activity levels. The inclusion criteria were studies published in English between 2000 and 2022, encompassing urban, suburban, and rural contexts. Thematic analysis was employed to categorise studies based on the specific aspects of the built environment they investigated (walking infrastructure, cycling infrastructure, parks and open spaces, and sports facilities) and the social determinants they examined. Results A total of 72 studies were included in the review, illustrating a multifaceted relationship between access to physical activity opportunities and social determinants such as socioeconomic status, community engagement, and urban design. The findings highlight the significant role of socioeconomic factors and the quality of PA infrastructure in promoting or hindering PA across communities. Effective urban planning was identified as crucial in providing expanded physical activity opportunities, notably through more pedestrian-friendly environments, comprehensive cycling infrastructure, and accessible green spaces and sports facilities. Conclusions This review emphasises the significant impact of socioeconomic status and urban planning on access to physical activity opportunities. This underscores the necessity for urban planning policies to adopt an inclusive approach, considering the varied needs of different population groups to ensure equitable access to physical activity resources. Such strategies are crucial for public health initiatives aimed at enhancing physical activity levels across diverse community sectors, offering a potential avenue to alleviate health disparities associated with inactivity.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Høyer-Kruse, Jens","Schmidt, Eva Berthelsen","Faber, Anne","Pedersen, Marlene Rosager Lund"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-760821"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Pedersen, Marlene Rosager Lund"],"keyword":["Built environment","City planning","Environment Design","Exercise","Health","Humans","Physical activity","Scoping review","Social Class","Social Environment","Socioeconomic Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the interplay between social environment and opportunities for physical activity within the built environment a scoping review","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-024-19733-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-760821","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11363614","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85202704284","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39215315","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-2118e09d-76c5-435b-8233-313cf26d2cbb"],"keyword":["Built environment","City Planning","Environment Design","Exercise","Health","Humans","Physical activity","Scoping review","Social Class","Social environment","Socioeconomic Factors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-69A35326F3853C51837252D70D096FC6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/271525704/s12889-024-19733-x.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-024-19733-x","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Centre for Sports, Health and Civil Society","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Active Living"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens","key":"høyerkrusepau","last":"Høyer-Kruse","locid":"5e059f63-1830-43ac-a9a2-7c0233032d2a","name":"Høyer-Kruse, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-0150-613X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Eva Berthelsen","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"0432a9ca-57e3-475c-b184-a5f8c0ac8f61","name":"Schmidt, Eva Berthelsen","orcid":"0000-0002-8726-5620","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"faberpau","last":"Faber","locid":"37446218-e236-4fa9-ad58-913ddfa87384","name":"Faber, Anne","orcid":"0000-0003-3347-8169","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marlene Rosager Lund","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"e47b826b-c800-48d3-90aa-3821fbe25a4c","name":"Pedersen, Marlene Rosager Lund","orcid":"0000-0002-1988-9597","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39215315"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12889-024-19733-x","issn":["14712458"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMC Public Health","paperid":"2361","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Public Health","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85202704284"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-2118e09d-76c5-435b-8233-313cf26d2cbb"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The interplay between social environment and opportunities for physical activity within the built environment: a scoping review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-760821","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This study explores the use of generative AI (GenAI) and research integrity assessments of use cases by researchers, including PhD students, at Danish universities. Conducted through a survey sent to all Danish researchers from January to February 2024, the study received 2,534 responses and evaluated 32 GenAI use cases across five research phases: idea generation, research design, data collection, data analysis, and writing/reporting. Respondents reported on their own and colleagues' GenAI usage. They also assessed whether the practices in the use cases were considered good research practice. Through an explorative factor analysis, we identified three clusters of perception: \"GenAI as a work horse,\" \"GenAI as a language assistant only\", and \"GenAI as a research accelerator\". The findings further show varied opinions on GenAI's research integrity implications. Language editing and data analysis were generally viewed positively, whereas experiment design and peer review tasks faced more criticism. Controversial areas included image creation/modification and synthetic data, with comments highlighting the need for critical and reflexive use of GenAI. Usage differed by main research area, with technical and quantitative sciences reporting slightly higher usage and more positive assessments. Junior researchers used GenAI more than senior colleagues, while no significant gender differences were observed.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Andersen, Jens Peter","Degn, Lise","Fishberg, Rachel","Graversen, Ebbe Krogh","Horbach, Serge Pascal Johannes M","Kalpazidou Schmidt, Evanthia","Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","Sørensen, Mads P."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-762695"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Jens Peter"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Radboud University Nijmegen"],"keyword":["GenAI","Generative Artificial Intellingence","Research integrity","Research practice","Research processes","Use cases"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"generative artificial intelligence genai in the research process a survey of researchers practices and perceptions","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dpr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/83whe","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-762695","ids":["au-0a2d4be7-0e9d-4fbd-b150-43f55cdc36d2"],"keyword":["GenAI","Generative Artificial Intellingence","Research integrity","Research practice","Research processes","Use cases"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7146F61A159855C9ABF544E1F40A9E15"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/83whe","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science - Danish Centre for Studies in Research and Research Policy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Peter","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"b1e70d8a-0883-480d-8c07-5a7775417377","name":"Andersen, Jens Peter","orcid":"0000-0003-2444-6210","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lise","key":"degnpau","last":"Degn","locid":"7c40c5f0-8571-476e-b8a6-6041c38e5e77","name":"Degn, Lise","orcid":"0000-0001-6571-9743","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rachel","key":"fishbergpau","last":"Fishberg","locid":"85c23222-c3c2-4915-8c7b-62b2e0f861cf","name":"Fishberg, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0002-4386-2933","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ebbe Krogh","key":"graversenpau","last":"Graversen","locid":"7ad5f98b-e0b1-48c6-a8f3-a9c413ea584f","name":"Graversen, Ebbe Krogh","orcid":"0000-0003-4009-2678","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Serge Pascal Johannes M","key":"horbachpau","last":"Horbach","name":"Horbach, Serge Pascal Johannes M","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Evanthia","key":"kalpazidouschmidtpau","last":"Kalpazidou Schmidt","locid":"30cd8ce5-47fe-495b-a57f-03e9c5e5eae6","name":"Kalpazidou Schmidt, Evanthia","orcid":"0000-0002-3204-0803","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Wiborg","key":"schneiderpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"8fcbf953-b83f-4fe7-b698-d4aec218e1f5","name":"Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","orcid":"0000-0001-5556-0919","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads P.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"fa411f34-079c-444b-afc9-a1ad05e462e3","name":"Sørensen, Mads P.","orcid":"0000-0003-2455-2515","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31235/osf.io/83whe","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-0a2d4be7-0e9d-4fbd-b150-43f55cdc36d2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in the research process – a survey of researchers’ practices and perceptions","type":"Preprint","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-762695","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Transcatheter patent foramen ovale (PFO) closure is the recommended treatment for patients age 18 to 60 years with cryptogenic stroke having a high probability of being PFO-related. Limited data exist on stroke recurrence after PFO closure outside clinical trials. Objectives: The purpose of this study was to examine stroke recurrence after PFO closure in routine clinical practice. Methods: We used nationwide population-based Danish registries to conduct a cohort study of all patients with PFO closure during 2008 to 2021 (n = 1,162) and a birth year and sex-matched comparison cohort from the general population (n = 11,620). We calculated absolute and relative risks of ischemic stroke within 4 years after PFO closure. We used weighted Cox regression to estimate adjusted HRs of the association between PFO closure vs the general population and ischemic stroke. Results: The absolute risks of ischemic stroke in patients with PFO closure and in the general population, respectively, were 1.4% (95% CI: 0.8%-2.3%) and 0.1% (95% CI: 0.0%-0.1%) at 1 year, 1.4% (95% CI: 0.8%-2.3%) and 0.2% (95% CI: 0.2%-0.4%) at 2 years, 2.2% (95% CI: 1.3%-3.5%) and 0.4% (95% CI: 0.2%-0.5%) at 3 years, and 2.5% (95% CI: 1.5%-4.0%) and 0.4% (95% CI: 0.3%-0.6%) at 4 years. Thus, the absolute 4-year risk of ischemic stroke was 2.1% (95% CI: 0.9%-3.3%) higher in patients with PFO closure than in the general population, corresponding to an adjusted HR of 6.3 (95% CI: 3.1-12.6). Conclusions: The 4-year risk of ischemic stroke after routine PFO closure for cryptogenic stroke was comparable to that observed in clinical trials, but remained higher than in the general population.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Bonnesen, Kasper","Korsholm, Kasper","Andersen, Asger","Pedersen, Lars","Simonsen, Claus Ziegler","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Schmidt, Morten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-763204"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Bonnesen, Kasper"],"keyword":["Cohort Studies","Epidemiology","Ischemic stroke","Patent foramen ovale"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk of ischemic stroke after patent foramen ovale closure","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85204371709","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-763204","identifier":[{"id":"39357939","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85204371709","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-78490daa-1cf5-46a8-bd52-87a69d7ea5f4"],"keyword":["cohort studies","epidemiology","ischemic stroke","patent foramen ovale"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-22F0938C02B9932AB69AFC30F3FFEB4F"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kasper","key":"bonnesenpau","last":"Bonnesen","locid":"08f71580-e725-41b2-9592-48981f741350","name":"Bonnesen, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0002-8626-2372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kasper","key":"korsholmpau","last":"Korsholm","locid":"47d8c195-2a81-4cbe-8d5a-1486606be651","name":"Korsholm, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-4724-0880","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"a0fdd247-3fb1-4c38-a1b2-f37d89f8f5c9","name":"Pedersen, Lars","orcid":"0000-0002-9341-3888","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Claus","key":"zieglersimonsenpau","last":"Ziegler Simonsen","locid":"ccf2fb52-6a4a-45b2-adb0-d6e83324879f","name":"Simonsen, Claus Ziegler","name_org":"Ziegler Simonsen, Claus","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"21e3a3e6-e25e-48cf-b243-b8f43dca3bb8","name":"Schmidt, Morten","orcid":"0000-0002-4935-4059","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39357939"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jacc.2024.07.015","issn":["07351097","15583597"],"issn_org":["07351097"],"issue":"15","journal":"Journal of the American College of Cardiology","pages":"1424-1433","publisher":"Elsevier Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of the American College of Cardiology","volume":"84"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85204371709"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-78490daa-1cf5-46a8-bd52-87a69d7ea5f4"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Risk of Ischemic Stroke After Patent Foramen Ovale Closure","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-763204","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A short-term hyperlocal meteorological forecast is an important tool for mitigating bridge risks as it empowers bridge operators and maintenance teams with timely and accurate weather predictions. Accurate predictions enable proactive responses to adverse conditions. Bridges are particularly vulnerable to extreme weather events, which can compromise their structural integrity and pose significant hazards. For instance, heavy rain can lead to flooding and erosion around bridge foundations, high winds can impose dynamic loads that strain the bridge’s superstructure, freezing temperatures can cause ice accumulation and expansion within concrete or steel components, and fog can severely limit visibility for drivers, increasing the likelihood of accidents. This study presents a short-term hyperlocal meteorological forecast framework designed to assist in the real-time decision-making processes on the bridge. The proposed framework leverages cutting-edge machine learning algorithms, integrating a blend of local and global sensor systems to continuously collect real-time data. The data also forms a comprehensive dataset for the training process. The capacity of the framework to provide real-time weather insights for one geographic location is evaluated. In this context, this study critically examines one potential application of the proposed framework in predicting wind gusts, scrutinizing its versatility and efficacy in addressing diverse weather-related challenges faced by bridge management. Furthermore, the potential of the framework to enhance the resilience of transportation networks by minimizing disruptions and structural damage caused by adverse weather events is discussed and quantified.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Georgakis, Christos Thomas","Lagaros, Nikos","Tsakalis, Pantelis","Chamatidis, Ilias","Demartino, Cristoforo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-772354"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Technical University of Athens","Roma Tre University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a short term hyperlocal meteorological forecast tool for mitigating bridge risks","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003483755-322","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-772354","identifier":[{"id":"85200382212","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-505bcbe5-f5d4-417c-9f8b-7079cc8581f4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A1E76BDBAC8F41F0A52E76038A1CE669"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003483755-322","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Structural Dynamics and Geotechnical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"national technical university of athens","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National Technical University of Athens","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"roma tre university","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Roma Tre University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christos Thomas","key":"georgakispau","last":"Georgakis","locid":"637b5801-43dd-43b2-aa14-ba18720b2ccf","name":"Georgakis, Christos Thomas","orcid":"0000-0001-6001-3856","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nikos","key":"lagarospau","last":"Lagaros","name":"Lagaros, Nikos","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pantelis","key":"tsakalispau","last":"Tsakalis","name":"Tsakalis, Pantelis","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilias","key":"chamatidispau","last":"Chamatidis","name":"Chamatidis, Ilias","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Cristoforo","key":"demartinopau","last":"Demartino","name":"Demartino, Cristoforo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jens","key":"sandagerjensenpedh","last":"Sandager Jensen","name":"Sandager Jensen, Jens","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Dan","key":"mfrangopolpedh","last":"M. Frangopol","name":"M. Frangopol, Dan","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jacob","key":"wittrupschmidtpedh","last":"Wittrup Schmidt","name":"Wittrup Schmidt, Jacob","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1201/9781003483755-322","eisbn":["9781003483755"],"isbn":["9781032770406"],"pages":"2710-2717","place":"London","publisher":"CRC Press","status":"p","title":"Bridge Maintenance, Safety, Management, Digitalization and Sustainability - Proceedings of the 12th International Conference on Bridge Maintenance, Safety and Management, IABMAS 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85200382212"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-505bcbe5-f5d4-417c-9f8b-7079cc8581f4"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"A short-term hyperlocal meteorological forecast tool for mitigating bridge risks","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-772354","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Natural seafloor depressions, known as pockmarks, are common subaqueous geomorphological features found from the deep ocean trenches to shallow lakes. Pockmarks can form rapidly or over millions of years and have a large variety of shapes created and maintained by a large variety of mechanisms. In the sandy sediments of the southeastern North Sea, abundant shallow pockmarks are ubiquitous and occur at shallow water depths (<50 m). Their formation has previously been linked to methane seepage from the seafloor. Here, we characterize over 50,000 pockmarks based on their morphology, geochemical signature, and the subsurface pre-conditions using a new integrated geoscientific data set, combining geophysical and sedimentological data with geochemical porewater and oceanographic analysis. We test whether the methane seepage is indeed responsible for pockmark formation. However, our data suggest that neither the seepage of light hydrocarbons nor groundwater is driving pockmark formation. Because of this lack of evidence for fluid seepage, we favor the previously suggested biotic formation but also discuss positive feedback mechanisms in ocean bottom currents as a formation process. Based on a comparison of pockmarks to the central and southeastern North Sea, we find that local lithology significantly affects pockmark morphology. Muddy lithologies favor the formation of larger, long-lived structures, while sandy lithologies lead to short-lived, small-scale structures that are large in area but with shallow incision depth. We conclude that pockmarks in sandy environments might have been overlooked globally due to their shallow incision depth and recommend reevaluating the role of hydrocarbon ebullition in pockmark formation.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Böttner, Christoph","Hoffmann, Jasper J.L.","Unverricht, Daniel","Schmidt, Mark","Spiegel, Timo","Geersen, Jacob","Müller, Thomas Harald","Karstens, Jens","Andresen, Katrine Juul","Sander, Lasse","Schneider von Deimling, Jens","Schmidt, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-772661"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Böttner, Christoph"],"country":["Germany","Malta"],"glorg":["Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research (Helmholz)","All Global Affiliations","GEOMAR Helmholtz Centre for Ocean Research Kiel","Kiel University","Landesamt für Landwirtschaft, Umwelt und ländliche Räume des Landes Schleswig-Holstein","Leibniz Institute for Baltic Sea Research","University of Malta"],"keyword":["Fluid flow","Geomorphology","Interdisciplinary","Lithology","North Sea"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the enigmatic pockmarks of the sandy southeastern north sea","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2024GC011837","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-772661","identifier":[{"id":"85209638517","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-6c29bbbb-c707-48fe-b481-964ebc727504"],"keyword":["North Sea","fluid flow","geomorphology","interdisciplinary","lithology","pockmarks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F7EC9B00FE01357147039E68F8C82FA0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2024GC011837","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Geoscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","use