inical Research in Cardiology","pages":"1276-1285","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH","status":"p","title":"Clinical Research in Cardiology","volume":"111"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134489269"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-482055d0-e5fc-4817-b8f6-aaedbcb005dd","ku-e7770359-be1c-4c12-ad1c-0a4c422d5850"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Clinical and echocardiographic risk factors for device-related thrombus after left atrial appendage closure : an analysis from the multicenter EUROC-DRT registry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.76-017903","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"High indoor temperatures in traditional finisher pig buildings may lead to deteriorating production results, increased pen fouling and increased respiration rate and rectal temperature. Floor cooling may improve the thermal conditions for pigs on solid floors during warm periods, and therefore the objective was to investigate whether floor cooling could lower the respiration rate (RR), increase the percentage of pigs located on the solid floor and reduce fouling. In a commercial finisher pig building in Denmark, ten pens were assigned floor cooling and ten pens served as controls. Water was continuously circulated in embedded pipes in the concrete solid floor and the water was cooled when both the return water from the floor and the outdoor air was warmer than set thresholds. These thresholds were adjusted during the investigation and for outdoor temperature it was either 20 or 18°C, and for return water flow 18, 20 or 22°C. The results showed that floor cooling significantly reduced RR and increased the percentage of pigs located on the solid floor at all three return flow temperatures. E.g. at return flow temperature 20°C, in the temperature interval [28;30.1], floor cooling reduced RR from 98 to 50 bpm (P < 0.0001) and increased the percentage of pigs located on the solid floor from 31.8% to 28.2% (P < 0.001). At return flow temperature 18°C and 20°C, the interaction between pen temperature and cooling was significant, hence floor cooling reduced the effect of the pen temperature. RR increased by 6.6 bpm when the pen temperature increased by 1°C in pens without floor cooling and by 1.9bpm in pens with floor cooling. Floor cooling did, however, not affect pen fouling where more than 25% of the solid floor was soiled in 13% of the observations in both cooled and uncooled pens.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Brandt, P.","Grønvig, M.","Rong, Li","Zhang, Guoqiang","Gautam, Khem Raj","Kristensen, Jens Kr","Bjerg, B."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-027502"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Bjerg, B."],"keyword":["Ambient temperature","Finishing pigs","Floor cooling","Heat stress","Respiration rate"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effect of floor cooling on respiration rate and distribution of pigs in the pen","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.livsci.2022.104832","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-027502","identifier":[{"id":"85123091380","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000791696300003","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-cc93a159-bdb6-4cb1-961b-7886fb529ee8","ku-34f8d45e-fde3-4f2f-9618-7ceb003f83ea"],"keyword":["Ambient temperature","Finishing pigs","Floor cooling","Heat stress","Respiration rate"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D673F575923D8834FE3952C09786EF9F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/333395425/1_s2.0_S1871141322000129_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/291126411/1_s2.0_S1871141322000129_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.livsci.2022.104832","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Production, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Design and Construction"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"P.","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"10c2aba4-4389-4a26-841c-42a5be20d649","name":"Brandt, Pia","name_org":"Brandt, P.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"M.","key":"grønvigpau","last":"Grønvig","locid":"e3005179-66f8-4e24-9715-a927d6ef6e26","name":"Grønvig, Matias","name_org":"Grønvig, M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"L.","key":"rongpau","last":"Rong","locid":"f946db2a-3da4-452d-92df-8633084731ea","name":"Rong, Li","name_org":"Rong, L.","orcid":"0000-0003-0622-3590","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"G.","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","locid":"2caa7f08-4340-484c-8f84-a84fab3b8cef","name":"Zhang, Guoqiang","name_org":"Zhang, G.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"K. R.","key":"gautampau","last":"Gautam","locid":"2ad9fafb-c0f5-4484-b1b2-fd066cb30d12","name":"Gautam, Khem Raj","name_org":"Gautam, K. R.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"J. K.","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"6916b52e-d565-4f5f-9fc0-5d58a825f54e","name":"Kristensen, Jens Kr","name_org":"Kristensen, J. K.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"B.","key":"bjergpau","last":"Bjerg","locid":"8e72047d-9f57-476c-b88f-64dcb66c4e62","name":"Bjerg, Bjarne Schmidt","name_org":"Bjerg, B.","orcid":"0000-0002-4032-3221","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.livsci.2022.104832","issn":["18711413","18780490"],"issn_org":["18711413"],"journal":"Livestock Science","paperid":"104832","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Livestock Science","volume":"257"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85123091380"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-cc93a159-bdb6-4cb1-961b-7886fb529ee8","ku-34f8d45e-fde3-4f2f-9618-7ceb003f83ea"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The effect of floor cooling on respiration rate and distribution of pigs in the pen","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-027502","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Retinal neurodegeneration is evident in early diabetic retinopathy (DR) which may be associated with other neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer's disease (AD). OBJECTIVE: To investigate diabetes and DR as a risk marker of present and incident AD. METHODS: A register-based cohort study was performed. We included 134,327 persons with diabetes above 60 years of age, who had attended DR screening, and 651,936 age- and gender-matched persons without diabetes. RESULTS: At baseline, the prevalence of AD was 0.7% and 1.3% among patients with and without diabetes, respectively. In a multivariable regression model, patients with diabetes were less likely to have AD at baseline (adjusted OR 0.63, 95% CI 0.59-0.68). During follow-up, incident AD was registered for 1473 (0.35%) and 6,899 (0.34%) persons with and without diabetes, respectively. Compared to persons without diabetes, persons with diabetes and no DR had a lower risk to develop AD (adjusted HR 0.87, 95% CI 0.81-0.93), while persons with diabetes and DR had higher risk of AD (adjusted HR 1.24, 95% CI 1.08-1.43). When persons with diabetes and no DR were used as references, a higher risk of incident AD was observed in persons with DR (adjusted HR 1.34, 95% CI 1.18-1.53). CONCLUSION: Individuals with diabetes without DR were less likely to develop AD compared to persons without diabetes. However, individuals with DR had a 34% higher risk of incident AD, which raise the question whether screening for cognitive impairment should be done among individuals with DR.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Pedersen, Frederik Nørregaard","Stokholm, Lonny","Pouwer, Frans","Hass Rubin, Katrine","Peto, Tunde","Frydkjær-Olsen, Ulrik","Thykjær, Anne Suhr","Andersen, Nis","Andresen, Jens","Bek, Toke","La Cour, Morten","Heegaard, Steffen","Højlund, Kurt","Kawasaki, Ryo","Hajari, Javad Nouri","Ohm Kyvik, Kirsten","Laugesen, Caroline Schmidt","Schielke, Katja Christina","Simó, Rafael","Grauslund, Jakob"],"cuid