Lone R.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"c598ecb0-8738-4464-8339-c7b8614e73ae","name":"Mortensen, Louise","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren T","key":"skoupau","last":"Skou","locid":"656a59cf-32bc-4190-b072-4adf47d954c1","name":"Skou, Søren Thorgaard","name_org":"Skou, Søren T","orcid":"0000-0003-4336-7059","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35642499"],"pub":{"doi":"10.2340/17453674.2022.2807","issn":["17453674","17453682"],"issn_org":["17453674"],"journal":"Acta Orthopaedica","pages":"509-518","publisher":"Medical Journals Sweden AB","status":"p","title":"Acta Orthopaedica","volume":"93"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131225779"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-32d9a6b4-6dc5-411a-a375-763b5fc641de","ku-8e05f75a-a4a9-48a3-a791-dd16f038ae15","sdu-78acfc6b-93b5-437c-a9a9-1552f6ce869e"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Feasibility, safety, and patient-reported outcomes 90 days after same-day total knee arthroplasty : a matched cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.56-016318","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Pulmonary embolism response teams (PERT) aim to improve treatment of acute pulmonary embolism (PE). PERT focus on intermediate- and high-risk PE patients, but recent multicenter studies show that low-risk PE patients compose one in five of all PERT cases. Conversely, not all intermediate- and high-risk PE patients elicit a PERT activation. The factors leading to PERT activations remain unknown. This study aims to describe the patient characteristics associated with PERT activation for low-risk PE patients and characteristics precluding PERT activation for intermediate/high-risk PE patients. We analysed data from all patients with confirmed PE diagnosed in the Massachusetts General Hospital Emergency Department from August 2013 to February 2017 and cross-referred these data with patients who received a PERT activation and patients who did not. Patients were stratified into low-risk or intermediate/high-risk PE. Univariate analyses were performed within each risk group comparing patients with a PERT activation and patients without. Fifteen percent (56/374) of low-risk PE patients triggered a PERT activation. Patient characteristics associated with PERT activation were: (1) vascular disease, (2) pulmonary diseases, (3) thrombophilia, (4) current use of anticoagulants, (5) central PE and (6) concurrent DVT. Thirty-five percent (110/283) of intermediate/high-risk PE patients did not elicit a PERT activation. Patient characteristics precluding a PERT activation were: (1) vascular disease, (2) malignancies and (3) asymptomatic presentation. Low-risk PE patients with PERT activations had more extensive clot burden, complex comorbidities, or had failed anticoagulation treatment. Intermediate/high-risk PE patients without PERT activations tended to have malignancies or vascular disease.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Kramer, Anders","Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","Giordano, Nicholas","Zheng, Hui","Andersen, Asger","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Kabrhel, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-200892"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Mortensen, Christian Schmidt"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University"],"keyword":["Acute care","Deep vein thrombosis","Emergency medicine","Pulmonary embolism","Rapid response team","Thromboembolism"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"predicting factors for pulmonary embolism response team activation in a general pulmonary embolism population","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85112041106","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-200892","identifier":[{"id":"85112041106","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000683251600003","type":"wos"},{"id":"34370168","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-7d03902e-9bbc-4204-8300-365acb299bb6"],"keyword":["Acute care","Deep vein thrombosis","Emergency medicine","Pulmonary embolism","Rapid response team","Thromboembolism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-14638597B62DE967EE770FA167D92D48"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"kramerpau","last":"Kramer","locid":"be3123b5-5f55-4c8a-930c-f0f39cbc2981","name":"Kramer, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-5546-8693","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Gammelgaard","key":"schultzpau","last":"Schultz","locid":"9825d55c-97ee-4bab-a003-801dcd6d6df8","name":"Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicholas","key":"giordanopau","last":"Giordano","name":"Giordano, Nicholas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Hui","key":"zhengpau","last":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hui","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"kabrhelpau","last":"Kabrhel","name":"Kabrhel, Christopher","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34370168"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s11239-021-02533-0","issn":["09295305","1573742X"],"issn_org":["09295305"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Thrombosis and Thrombolysis","pages":"506-513","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Thrombosis and Thrombolysis","volume":"53"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112041106"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7d03902e-9bbc-4204-8300-365acb299bb6"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Predicting factors for pulmonary embolism response team activation in a general pulmonary embolism population","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-200892","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Validation of the 2016 RANO MRI scorecard for leptomeningeal metastasis failed for multiple reasons. Accordingly, this joint EORTC Brain Tumor Group and RANO effort sought to prospectively validate a revised MRI scorecard for response assessment in leptomeningeal metastasis. METHODS: Coded paired cerebrospinal MRI of 20 patients with leptomeningeal metastases from solid cancers at baseline and follow-up after treatment and instructions for assessment were provided via the EORTC imaging platform. The Kappa coefficient was used to evaluate the interobserver pairwise agreement. RESULTS: Thirty-five raters participated, including 9 neuroradiologists, 17 neurologists, 4 radiation oncologists, 3 neurosurgeons, and 2 medical oncologists. Among single leptomeningeal metastases-related imaging findings at baseline, the best median concordance was noted for hydrocephalus (Kappa = 0.63), and the worst median concordance for spinal linear enhancing disease (Kappa = 0.46). The median concordance of raters for the overall response assessment was moderate (Kappa = 0.44). Notably, the interobserver agreement for the presence of parenchymal brain metastases at baseline was fair (Kappa = 0.29) and virtually absent for their response to treatment. 394 of 700 ratings (20 patients x 35 raters, 56%) were fully completed. In 308 of 394 fully completed ratings (78%), the overall response assessment perfectly matched the summary interpretation of the single ratings as proposed in the scorecard instructions. CONCLUSION: This study confirms the principle utility of the new scorecard, but also indicates the need for training of MRI assessment with a dedicated reviewer panel in clinical trials. Electronic case report forms with \"blocking options\" may be required to enforce completeness and quality of scoring.","author_count":38,"contributors":["Le Rhun, Emilie","Devos, Patrick","Winklhofer, Sebastian","Lmalem, Hafida","Brandsma, Dieta","Kumthekar, Priya","Castellano, Antonella","Compter, Annette","Dhermain, Frederic","Franceschi, Enrico","Forsyth, Peter","Furtner, Julia","Galldiks, Norbert","Gállego Pérez-Larraya, Jaime","Gempt, Jens","Hattingen, Elke","Hempel, Johann Martin","Lukacova, Slávka","Minniti, Giuseppe","O'Brien, Barbara","Postma, Tjeerd J.","Roth, Patrick","Rudà, Roberta","Schaefer, Niklas","Schmidt, Nils O.","Snijders, Tom J.","Thust, Steffi","van den Bent, Martin","van der Hoorn, Anouk","Vogin, Guillaume","Smits, Marion","Tonn, Joerg C.","Jaeckle, Kurt A.","Preusser, Matthias","Glantz, Michael","Wen, Patrick Y.","Bendszus, Martin","Weller, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-247076"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Le Rhun, Emilie"],"country":["Austria","Belgium","France","Germany","Italy","Netherlands","Spain","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre François Baclesse","Centre Oscar Lambret","Erasmus University Rotterdam","European Organisation for Research and Treatment of Cancer Data Center","Goethe University Frankfurt","Harvard University","Heidelberg University ","IRCCS Istituto delle Scienze Neurologiche di Bologna","Ludwig Maximilian University","Mayo Clinic Jacksonville, FL","Medical University of Vienna","Netherlands Cancer Institute","Northwestern University","Pennsylvania State University","Technical University of Munich","Universite Paris-Saclay","University College London","University of Bonn","University of Cologne","University of Groningen","University of Navarra","University of Regensburg","University of Siena","University of South Florida","University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center","University of Turin","University of Tübingen","University of Zurich","Université de Lille","Utrecht University","VU University Amsterdam","Vita-Salute San Raffaele University"],"keyword":["Brain","Brain Ne