":"Elsevier Ireland Ltd","status":"p","title":"Resuscitation","volume":"175"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85129770063"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","prehospital emergency medical services"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-2f4d42f1-7e14-4934-a12b-0c2f1b103f55","au-ca4e2548-c968-4347-929e-d3dc08d3e5bb","ku-b3e35d64-a999-4717-9cd5-0186b3394885","sdu-11604bf5-280e-45a2-bd13-340fdf50ad90"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Effect of Vasopressin and Methylprednisolone vs. Placebo on Long-Term Outcomes in Patients with In-Hospital Cardiac Arrest A Randomized Clinical Trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-099.05-002368","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Respiration changes intrathoracic pressure and lung volumes in a cyclic manner, which affect cardiac function. Invasive ventricular pressure-volume (PV) loops can be recorded during ongoing mechanical ventilation or in transient apnea. No consensus exists considering ventilatory mode during PV loop recording. The objective of this study was to investigate the magnitude of any systematic difference of bi-ventricular PV loop variables recorded during mechanical ventilation versus apnea. PV loops were recorded simultaneously from the right ventricle and left ventricle in a closed chest porcine model during mechanical ventilation and in transient apnea (n=72). Variables were compared by regression analyses. Mechanical ventilation versus apnea affected regression coefficients for important PV variables including right ventricular stroke volume (1.22, 95% CI [1.08-1.36], p=0.003), right ventricular ejection fraction (0.90, 95% CI [0.81-1.00], p=0.043) and right ventricular arterial elastance (0.61, 95%CI [0.55-0.68], p<0.0001). Right ventricular pressures and volumes were parallelly shifted with Y-intercepts different from 0. Few left ventricular variables were affected, mainly first derivatives of pressure (dP/dt(max): 0.96, 95% CI [0.92-0.99], p=0.016, and dP/dt(min): 0.92, 95% CI [0.86-0.99], p=0.026), which might be due to decreased heart rate in apnea (Y-intercept -6.88, 95% CI [-12.22; -1.54], p=0.012). We conclude, that right ventricular stroke volume, ejection fraction and arterial elastance were mostly affected by apnea compared to mechanical ventilation.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lyhne, Mads Dam","Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Hansen, Jacob Valentin","Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-182154"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Lyhne, Mads Dam"],"keyword":["Animals","Heart Ventricles","Respiration Artificial adverse effects","Stroke Volume physiology","Swine","Ventricular Function Left physiology","Ventricular Function Right","Ventricular Pressure"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effects of mechanical ventilation versus apnea on bi ventricular pressure volume loop recording","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85128000656","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-182154","identifier":[{"id":"85128000656","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35043651","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8997677","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-5c94215d-c326-41a6-9b05-5595fee188bd"],"keyword":["Animals","Apnea/diagnosis","Heart Ventricles","Respiration, Artificial/adverse effects","Stroke Volume/physiology","Swine","Ventricular Function, Left/physiology","Ventricular Function, Right","Ventricular Pressure"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1528A4F09A6FFDB0A4C70DC606E4C894"],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Dam","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Valentin","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"31e33719-b4f4-428d-8342-33c42a4c78c2","name":"Hansen, Jacob Valentin","orcid":"0000-0002-7017-575X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Simone Juel","key":"dragsbaekpau","last":"Dragsbaek","locid":"9c395385-9047-41cb-9a96-a1cdc538fdcf","name":"Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35043651"],"pub":{"doi":"10.33549/physiolres.934787","issn":["08628408","18029973"],"issn_org":["08628408"],"issue":"1","journal":"Physiological Research","pages":"103-111","publisher":"Czech Academy of Sciences","status":"p","title":"Physiological Research","volume":"71"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85128000656"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-5c94215d-c326-41a6-9b05-5595fee188bd"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Effects of mechanical ventilation versus apnea on bi-ventricular pressure-volume loop recording","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-182154","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Research summary We present a framework for theory-based learning and experimentation under uncertainty. Strategists' assumptions about how an envisioned future can be reached are likely incomplete and possibly wrong, for instance, if critical contingencies have been overlooked. We explain how strategists can learn from thinking about and testing necessary conditions for an envisioned future to materialize. By logically linking assumptions to consequences our framework allows drawing inferences from experiments with testable assumptions about elements of a strategy that cannot be tested without major investments. Our framework contains the first formal model of learning from arguments in the strategy literature. By using our framework, strategists can maintain focus on an envisioned future while at the same time systematically seeking out reasons and evidence for why they are wrong. Managerial summary We develop a framework that helps strategists to learn and understand what it takes to reach ambitious goals when there is substantial uncertainty. We ask strategists to formulate their assumptions as a theory: what needs to be true for their goal to materialize. Our framework enables strategists to scrutinize and improve their assumptions by raising objections against their theory and by pointing them to critical experiments to learn whether their assumptions hold. Using our results, strategists can in particular identify overlooked critical contingencies. Overall, we suggest how strategists should revise their beliefs about what it takes to be successful in the light of evidence and arguments for and against their strategy.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Ehrig, Timo","Schmidt, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-294485"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Schmidt, Jens"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Belief revision","Experimentation","Learning","Managerial theories","Uncertainty"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"theory based learning and experimentation how strategists can systematically generate knowledge at the edge between the known and the unknown","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3381","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Since writing the first draft this article has benefitted from comments and conversations with many people about which we are very grateful. We would like to in particular thank our editor Olivier Chatain and two anonymous reviewers whose comments have greatly helped improve the article. We also want to thank Sharwin Rezagholi, Jukka Luoma, and Joosef Valli for helpful comments and suggestions."],"id":"S01-000.00-294485","identifier":[{"id":"85124610067","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-c8b3e1f4-cdc6-4f51-b306-8ec90d9efff1"],"keyword":["Belief revision","Experimentation","Learning","Managerial theories","Uncertainty"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-07C65B0C502EFF5C7AFCB0A468D86A64"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/94390511/timo_ehrig_et_al_theory_based_learning_and_experimentation_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3381","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Strategy and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Timo","key":"ehrigpau","last":"Ehrig","locid":"cca2661b-d9ba-419c-952e-397c9dc771af","name":"Ehrig, Timo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/smj.3381","issn":["01432095","10970266"],"issn_org":["01432095"],"issue":"7","journal":"Strategic Management Journal","pages":"1287-1318","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Ltd","status":"p","title":"Strategic Management Journal","volume":"43"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124610067"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-c8b3e1f4-cdc6-4f51-b306-8ec90d9efff1"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Theory-based Learning and Experimentation : How Strategists Can Systematically Generate Knowledge at the Edge between the Known and the Unknown","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-294485","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"There is a gap in the research literature with respect to explaining why some failing IT projects are allowed to continue instead of being terminated. Part of the problem is a gap with respect to an accepted general definition of IT project failure. This is significant because varying definition of IT project failure gives rise to misunderstandings in research and practice. The use of the derogative term “failure” is in itself problematic. This article gives a) an overview of what is meant by IT project failure in the literature, b) an updated set of IT project performance criteria associated with the attribution of failure, and c) a new argument for the theoretical predictability of IT project termination - the marginal cost trap. The article furthermore presents d) an outline of how these findings can contribute to preventing IT project failure. The research methods are a hermeneutic literature review (content analysis), small-n case studies (plausibility probes), and abductive theory generation.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Schmidt, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382059"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["IT project failure","IT project management","IT project performance","IT project termination","Information technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"it project failure termination and the marginal cost trap","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/1019255/JMPM03020","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382059","ids":["itu-35d81cae-3bdb-4d68-9386-47c3992238c8"],"keyword":["IT project failure","IT project management","IT project performance","IT project termination","information technology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E1716B66DD005F005C03D35CA0E7C583"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://journalmodernpm.com/manuscript/index.php/jmpm/article/view/536/479","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/1019255/JMPM03020","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333658/15_JMPM-494-ITProjectPerformanceAndTerminationJMPMv2.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"e0edf6ca-5a14-41d8-81cc-77fd67f03ee5","name":"Schmidt, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-2500-0467","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["23173963"],"issue":"30","journal":"Journal of Modern Project Management","pages":"255-275","paperid":"1019255/JMPM03020","status":"p","title":"Journal of Modern Project Management","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-35d81cae-3bdb-4d68-9386-47c3992238c8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"IT project failure, termination, and the marginal cost trap","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382059","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: Responses to COVID-19 vaccination in patients with chronic pulmonary diseases are poorly characterised. We aimed to describe humoral responses following two doses of BNT162b2 mRNA COVID-19 vaccine and identify risk factors for impaired responses. METHODS: Prospective cohort study including adults with chronic pulmonary diseases and healthcare personnel as controls (1:1). Blood was sampled at inclusion, 3 weeks, 2 and 6 months after first vaccination. We reported antibody concentrations as geometric means with 95% CI of receptor binding domain (RBD)-IgG and neutralising antibody index of inhibition of ACE-2/RBD interaction (%). A low responder was defined as neutralising index in the lowest quartile (primary outcome) or RBD-IgG <225 AU/mL plus neutralising index <25% (secondary outcome), measured at 2 months. We tested associations using Poisson regression. RESULTS: We included 593 patients and 593 controls, 75% of all had neutralising index ≥97% at 2 months. For the primary outcome, 34.7% of patients (n=157/453) and 12.9% of controls (n=46/359) were low responders (p<0.0001). For the secondary outcome, 8.6% of patients (n=39/453) and 1.4% of controls (n=5/359) were low responders (p<0.001). Risk factors associated with low responder included increasing age (per decade, adjusted risk ratio (aRR) 1.17, 95% CI 1.03 to 1.32), Charlson Comorbidity Index (per point) (aRR 1.15, 95% CI 1.05 to 1.26), use of prednisolone (aRR 2.08, 95% CI 1.55 to 2.77) and other immunosuppressives (aRR 2.21, 95% CI 1.65 to 2.97). DISCUSSION: Patients with chronic pulmonary diseases established functional humoral responses to vaccination, however lower than controls. Age, comorbidities and immunosuppression were associated with poor immunological responses.","author_count":30,"contributors":["Harboe, Zitta Barrella","Hamm, Sebastian Rask","Pérez-Alós, Laura","Sivapalan, Pradeesh","Priemé, Helene","Wilcke, Torgny","Kjeldgaard, Peter","Shaker, Saher Burhan","Svorre Jo