{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"Xavier","key":"freixaxavierpau","last":"Freixa","name":"Freixa, Xavier","rank":68,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher R.","key":"ellischristopherrpau","last":"Ellis","name":"Ellis, Christopher R.","rank":69,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"35","corresponding":null,"first":"Laurent","key":"fauchierlaurentpau","last":"Fauchier","name":"Fauchier, Laurent","rank":70,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Marcin","key":"demkowmarcinpau","last":"Demkow","name":"Demkow, Marcin","rank":71,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Horst","key":"sieverthorstpau","last":"Sievert","name":"Sievert, Horst","rank":72,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael L.","key":"mainmichaellpau","last":"Main","name":"Main, Michael L.","rank":73,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Benjamin","key":"hibbertbenjaminpau","last":"Hibbert","name":"Hibbert, Benjamin","rank":74,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"David R.","key":"holmesdavidrpau","last":"Holmes","name":"Holmes, David R.","rank":75,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mohamad","key":"alkhoulimohamadpau","last":"Alkhouli","name":"Alkhouli, Mohamad","rank":76,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34294267"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jacc.2021.04.098","issn":["07351097","15583597"],"issn_org":["07351097"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of the American College of Cardiology","pages":"297-313","publisher":"Elsevier Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of the American College of Cardiology","volume":"78"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85109533701"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ea83f5e2-1539-4761-9e72-9bc1af9a7e2f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Predictors of Device-Related Thrombus Following Percutaneous Left Atrial Appendage Occlusion","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-263195","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Recently, Kawarabayashi and Thorup presented the first deterministic edge-connectivity recognition algorithm in near-linear time. A crucial step in their algorithm uses the existence of vertex subsets of a simple graph G on n vertices whose contractions leave a multigraph with Õ(n∕δ) vertices and Õ(n) edges that preserves all non-trivial min-cuts of G, where δ is the minimum degree of G and Õ hides logarithmic factors. We present a simple argument that improves this contraction-based sparsifier by eliminating the poly-logarithmic factors, that is, we show a contraction-based sparsification that leaves O(n∕δ) vertices and O(n) edges, preserves all non-trivial min-cuts and can be computed in near-linear time Õ(m), where m is the number of edges of G. We also obtain that every simple graph has O((n∕δ)2) non-trivial min-cuts. Our approach allows to represent all non-trivial min-cuts of a graph by a cactus representation, whose cactus graph has O(n∕δ) vertices. Moreover, this cactus representation can be derived directly from the standard cactus representation of all min-cuts in linear time. We apply this compact structure to show that all min-cuts can be explicitly listed in Õ(m)+O(n2∕δ) time for every simple graph, which improves the previous best time bound O(nm) given by Gusfield and Naor.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lo, On Hei S.","Schmidt, Jens M.","Thorup, Mikkel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-544836"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["China","Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ilmenau University of Technology","University of Science and Technology of China"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"compact cactus representations of all non trivial min cuts","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/239808854/OA_Compact_Cactus_Representations_of_all_Non_Trivial_Min_Cuts.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-544836","identifier":[{"id":"85082857967","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-fdba2eb7-ee13-453a-bc12-9f23d1ed53c5"],"keyword":["Cactus representation","Contraction-based sparsification","DAG representation","Min-cuts enumeration","Non-trivial min-cuts"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1F2F352A991DB6516537E238BA4DAEB9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/239808854/OA_Compact_Cactus_Representations_of_all_Non_Trivial_Min_Cuts.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"university of science and technology of china","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"University of Science and Technology of China","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"ilmenau university of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ilmenau University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Complexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"On Hei S.","key":"lopau","last":"Lo","name":"Lo, On Hei S.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jens M.","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Jens M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Mikkel","key":"thoruppau","last":"Thorup","locid":"9a753f32-9b71-4058-8b1e-a94b71903f10","name":"Thorup, Mikkel","orcid":"0000-0001-5237-1709","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.dam.2020.03.046","issn":["0166218X","18726771"],"issn_org":["0166218X"],"journal":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","pages":"296-304","status":"p","title":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","volume":"303"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85082857967"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-fdba2eb7-ee13-453a-bc12-9f23d1ed53c5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Compact cactus representations of all non-trivial min-cuts","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-544836","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"With rising environmental concerns on the use of conventional, non-renewably sourced plastics, alternatives such as bioplastics and their impacts on the environment have been studied to replace the use of conventional plastics. In this study, life-cycle-assessment (LCA) was performed on a novel plastic, namely, whey plastic, i.e. plastic produced from whey protein, which is a residue of cheese-making process. Cradle-to-gate analysis was performed using the CML Baseline Method in SimaPro software. The results showed that for each 1000 kg of whey plastic produced, 2900 MJ of energy was consumed, 115.3 kg CO2 was emitted, 82.5 kg NOx was emitted, and it had the lowest global warming potential, freshwater, and marine aquatic toxicities, compared to other conventional plastics. Sensitivity analyses include transportation and land use changes were considered. Copolymer PEGMA was found to be a major contributor of environmental damages, hence replacement or modification of the chemical was suggested for future studies.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Chalermthai, Bushra","Giwa, Adewale","Schmidt, Jens Ejbye","Taher, Hanifa Al Blooshi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-581418"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East"],"corresponding_author":["Chalermthai, Bushra"],"country":["United Arab Emirates"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Khalifa University"],"keyword":["Bioplastic","Environmental impacts","LCA","Polymer","Whey"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"life cycle assessment of bioplastic production from whey protein obtained from dairy residues","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85107833864","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Khalifa University, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates is appreciated for providing the software and library resources for this work, which is part of PhD thesis by the first author."],"id":"S01-000.00-581418","identifier":[{"id":"85107833864","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-1cad68f8-105a-447d-8116-826e4d563f3d"],"keyword":["Bioplastic","Environmental impacts","LCA","Polymer","Whey"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-585EC75A7A836952792BC3B8CACFFEAE"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ae","key":"khalifa university","loc":["United Arab Emirates","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Khalifa University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Green Technology (IGT)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bushra","key":"chalermthaipau","last":"Chalermthai","name":"Chalermthai, Bushra","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adewale","key":"giwapau","last":"Giwa","name":"Giwa, Adewale","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Ejbye","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"14ca7f5b-030e-46a8-8aef-a09f6fa018f5","name":"Schmidt, Jens Ejbye","orcid":"0000-0001-6067-5032","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanifa Al Blooshi","key":"taherpau","last":"Taher","name":"Taher, Hanifa Al Blooshi","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.biteb.2021.100695","issn":["2589014X"],"journal":"Bioresource Technology Reports","paperid":"100695","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Bioresource Technology Reports","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85107833864"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-1cad68f8-105a-447d-8116-826e4d563f3d"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Life cycle assessment of bioplastic production from whey protein obtained from dairy residues","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-581418","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The heuristics strategists use to make predictions about key decision variables are often learned from only a small sample of observations, which leads to a risk of inappropriate generalization when strategists misjudge regularities. Building on the statistical learning literature, we show how strategists can mitigate this risk. Strategies to learn heuristics that accept a bias, that is, a systematic deviation of predictions from actual outcomes, can outperform unbiased strategies because they can reduce the variance component of prediction error: the degree to which random fluctuations in observational data are inappropriately generalized. We demonstrate how strategists who are aware of the trade-off between bias and variance can learn heuristics more effectively if they are also aware of the relevant characteristics of their learning environment. We discuss the implications of our results for our understanding of heuristics, (dynamic) capabilities, and managerial cognitive capabilities, and we outline opportunities for empirical work.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Ehrig, Timo","Schmidt, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-627812"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Schmidt, Jens"],"country":["Finland","Germany"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute for Mathematics in the Sciences"],"keyword":["Biases","Heuristics","Learning","Simple rules","Strategic decision making"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"making biased but better predictions the trade offs strategists face when they learn and use heuristics","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/200115068/EhrigSchmidtSO2019_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-627812","identifier":[{"id":"85074045118","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-be766513-7352-44a5-9da0-0d77d0ca3d42"],"keyword":["biases","heuristics","inappropriate generalization","learning","simple rules","strategic decision-making"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2D98F8E1E975918552EF70FFBB94F985"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/200115068/EhrigSchmidtSO2019_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Business & Management (DBM)","SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Business & Management (DBM), Strategic Organization Design (SOD)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"max planck institute for mathematics in the sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute for Mathematics in the Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Timo","key":"ehrigpau","last":"Ehrig","locid":"1d93dcf3-d076-4b92-a850-b859b652c167","name":"Ehrig, Timo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/1476127019869646","issn":["14761270","1741315X"],"issn_org":["14761270"],"issue":"2","journal":"Strategic Organization","pages":"263-284","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"p","title":"Strategic Organization","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85074045118"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-be766513-7352-44a5-9da0-0d77d0ca3d42"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Making biased but better predictions : The trade-offs strategists face when they learn and use heuristics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-627812","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"To meet the ambitious objectives of biodiversity and climate conventions, the international community requires clarity on how these objectives can be operationalized spatially and how multiple targets can be pursued concurrently. To support goal setting and the implementation of international strategies and action plans, spatial guidance is needed to identify which land areas have the potential to generate the greatest synergies between conserving biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. Here we present results from a joint optimization that minimizes the number of threatened species, maximizes carbon retention and water quality regulation, and ranks terrestrial conservation priorities globally. We found that selecting the top-ranked 30% and 50% of terrestrial land area would conserve respectively 60.7% and 85.3% of the estimated total carbon stock and 66% and 89.8% of all clean water, in addition to meeting conservation targets for 57.9% and 79% of all species considered. Our data and prioritization further suggest that adequately conserving all species considered (vertebrates and plants) would require giving conservation attention to ~70% of the terrestrial land surface. If priority was given to biodiversity only, managing 30% of optimally located land area for conservation may be sufficient to meet conservation targets for 81.3% of the terrestrial plant and vertebrate species considered. Our results provide a global assessment of where land could be optimally managed for conservation. We discuss how such a spatial prioritization framework can support the implementation of the biodiversity and climate conventions.","author_count":53,"contributors":["Jung, Martin","Arnell, Andy","de Lamo, Xavier","García-Rangel, Shaenandhoa","Lewis, Matthew","Mark, Jennifer","Merow, Cory","Miles, Lera","Ondo, Ian","Pironon, Samuel","Ravilious, Corinna","Rivers, Malin","Schepashenko, Dmitry","Tallowin, Oliver","van Soesbergen, Arnout","Govaerts, Rafaël","Boyle, Bradley L.","Enquist, Brian J.","Feng, Xiao","Gallagher, Rachael","Maitner, Brian","Meiri, Shai","Mulligan, Mark","Ofer, Gali","Roll, Uri","Hanson, Jeffrey O.","Jetz, Walter","Di Marco, Moreno","McGowan, Jennifer","Rinnan, D. Scott","Sachs, Jeffrey D.","Lesiv, Myroslava","Adams, Vanessa M.","Andrew, Samuel C.","Burger, Joseph R.","Hannah, Lee","Marquet, Pablo A.","McCarthy, James K.","Morueta-Holme, Naia","Newman, Erica A.","Park, Daniel S.","Roehrdanz, Patrick R.","Svenning, Jens Christian","Violle, Cyrille","Wieringa, Jan J.","Wynne, Graham","Fritz, Steffen","Strassburg, Bernardo B.N.","Obersteiner, Michael","Kapos, Valerie","Burgess, Neil","Schmidt-Traub, Guido","Visconti, Piero"],"cuid":["S02-094.84-018742"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Jung, Martin"],"country":["Australia","Austria","Brazil","Chile","France","Israel","Italy","Netherlands","New Zealand","Portugal","Russia","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ben-Gurion University of the Negev","Botanic Gardens Conservation International","CSIRO","Columbia University","Conservation International (USA)","Florida State University","Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis, Laxenburg","King's College London","Landcare Research","Macquarie University","Naturalis National Museum of Natural History","Pontificia Universidad Catolica de Chile","Pontificia Universidade Catolica do Rio de Janeiro","Purdue University","Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","Santa Fe Institute","Siberian Federal University","Tel Aviv University","The Nature Conservancy","Universidad de Chile","Universidade Federal do Rio de Janeiro","Universite de Montpellier","University of Arizona","University of Cambridge","Unive