{"abstract":"Pressure-volume (PV) loop recording enables the state-of-the-art investigation of load-independent variables of ventricular performance. Uni-ventricular evaluation is often performed in preclinical research. However, the right and left ventricles exert functional interdependence due to their parallel and serial connections, encouraging simultaneous evaluation of both ventricles. Furthermore, various pharmacological interventions may affect the ventricles and their preloads and afterloads differently. We describe our closed chest approach to admittance-based bi-ventricular PV loop recordings in a porcine model of acute right ventricular (RV) overload. We utilize minimally invasive techniques with all vascular accesses guided by ultrasound. PV catheters are positioned, under fluoroscopic guidance, to avoid thoracotomy in animals, as the closed chest approach maintains the relevant cardiopulmonary physiology. The admittance technology provides real-time PV loop recordings without the need for post-hoc processing. Furthermore, we explain some essential troubleshooting steps during critical timepoints of the presented procedure. The presented protocol is a reproducible and physiologically relevant approach to obtain a bi-ventricular cardiac PV loop recording in a large animal model. This can be applied to a large variety of cardiovascular animal research.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Lyhne, Mads Dam","Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","Hansen, Jacob Valentin","Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Kramer, Anders","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-130611"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Dysfunction","Echocardiography","Heart failure","INDEX","Performance","Quantitative assessment"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"closed chest biventricular pressure volume loop recordings with admittance catheters in a porcine model","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85107235056","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-130611","identifier":[{"id":"000658340400096","type":"wos"},{"id":"85107235056","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34096927","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-02643a0a-c684-4857-8dca-5576d6b9d0e3"],"keyword":["DYSFUNCTION","ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY","HEART-FAILURE","INDEX","LEFT-VENTRICULAR CONTRACTILITY","PERFORMANCE","QUANTITATIVE ASSESSMENT"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-0970083DFD2EF1FE87EF54F2EC1AF00C"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Dam","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Gammelgaard","key":"schultzpau","last":"Schultz","locid":"9825d55c-97ee-4bab-a003-801dcd6d6df8","name":"Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Simone Juel","key":"dragsbækpau","last":"Dragsbæk","locid":"9c395385-9047-41cb-9a96-a1cdc538fdcf","name":"Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","name_org":"Dragsbæk, Simone Juel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Valentin","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"31e33719-b4f4-428d-8342-33c42a4c78c2","name":"Hansen, Jacob Valentin","orcid":"0000-0002-7017-575X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Dahl","key":"kramerpau","last":"Kramer","locid":"be3123b5-5f55-4c8a-930c-f0f39cbc2981","name":"Kramer, Anders","name_org":"Kramer, Anders Dahl","orcid":"0000-0002-5546-8693","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34096927"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3791/62661","issn":["1940087X"],"journal":"Journal of Visualized Experiments","paperid":"e62661","publisher":"MyJoVE Corporation","status":"p","title":"Journal of Visualized Experiments","volume":"171"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85107235056"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-02643a0a-c684-4857-8dca-5576d6b9d0e3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Closed Chest Biventricular Pressure-Volume Loop Recordings with Admittance Catheters in a Porcine Model","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-130611","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: The haemodynamic response following acute, intermediate-risk pulmonary embolism is not well described. We aimed to describe the cardiovascular changes in the initial, critical phase 0-12 hours after acute pulmonary embolism in an in-vivo porcine model. Methods: Pigs were randomly allocated to pulmonary embolism (n = 6) or sham (n = 6). Pulmonary embolism was administered as autologous blood clots (20 × 1 cm) until doubling of mean pulmonary arterial pressure or mean pulmonary arterial pressure was greater than 34 mmHg. Sham animals received saline. Cardiopulmonary changes were evaluated for 12 hours after intervention by biventricular pressure-volume loop recordings, invasive pressure measurements, arterial and central venous blood gas analyses. Results: Mean pulmonary arterial pressure increased (P < 0.0001) and stayed elevated for 12 hours in the pulmonary embolism group compared to sham. Pulmonary vascular resistance and right ventricular arterial elastance (right ventricular afterload) were increased in the first 11 and 6 hours, respectively, after pulmonary embolism (P < 0.01 for both) compared to sham. Right ventricular ejection fraction was reduced (P < 0.01) for 8 hours, whereas a near-significant reduction in right ventricular stroke volume was observed (P = 0.06) for 4 hours in the pulmonary embolism group compared to sham. Right ventricular ventriculo-arterial coupling was reduced (P < 0.05) for 6 hours following acute pulmonary embolism despite increased right ventricular mechanical work in the pulmonary embolism group (P < 0.01) suggesting right ventricular failure. Conclusions: In a porcine model of intermediate-risk pulmonary embolism, the increased right ventricular afterload caused initial right ventricular ventriculo-arterial uncoupling and dysfunction. After approximately 6 hours, the right ventricular afterload returned to pre-pulmonary embolism values and right ventricular function improved despite a sustained high pulmonary arterial pressure. These results suggest an initial critical and vulnerable phase of acute pulmonary embolism before haemodynamic adaptation.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lyhne, Mads Dam","Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","Kramer, Anders","Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-181999"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animal model","Dysfunction","Gas exchange","Porcine model","Pulmonary circulation","Pulmonary embolism PE","Pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular function"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"right ventricular adaptation in the critical phase after acute intermediate risk pulmonary embolism","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85164543649","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-181999","identifier":[{"id":"85164543649","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33609136","type":"pmid"},{"id":"32436719","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000534765600001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-5c4e5586-8e1e-4e3d-9f3f-d03b69b3cbd8"],"keyword":["DYSFUNCTION","PORCINE MODEL","Pulmonary embolism (PE)","Pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular afterload","animal model","gas exchange","pulmonary circulation","right ventricular function"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AC9C628CBFFA893044C65803F9468D06"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Dam","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Gammelgaard","key":"schultzpau","last":"Schultz","locid":"9825d55c-97ee-4bab-a003-801dcd6d6df8","name":"Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"kramerpau","last":"Kramer","locid":"be3123b5-5f55-4c8a-930c-f0f39cbc2981","name":"Kramer, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-5546-8693","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32436719","33609136"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2048872620925253","issn":["20488726","20488734"],"issn_org":["20488726"],"issue":"3","journal":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","pages":"243-249","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164543649"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-5c4e5586-8e1e-4e3d-9f3f-d03b69b3cbd8"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Right ventricular adaptation in the critical phase after acute intermediate-risk pulmonary embolism","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-181999","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To investigate if oxygen could unload the right ventricle and improve right ventricle function in a porcine model mimicking intermediate-high risk acute pulmonary embolism. DESIGN: Controlled, blinded, animal study. SETTING: Tertiary university hospital, animal research laboratory. SUBJECTS: Female, Danish pigs (n = 16, approximately 60 kg). INTERVENTIONS: Acute autologous pulmonary embolism was induced until doubling of baseline mean pulmonary arterial pressure. Group 1 animals (n = 8) received increasing FIO2 (40%, 60%, and 100%) for time intervals of 15 minutes returning to atmospheric air between each level of FIO2. In group 2 (n = 8), the effects of FIO2 40% maintained over 75 minutes were studied. In both groups, pulmonary vasodilatation from inhaled nitric oxide (40 parts per million) was used as a positive control. MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS: Effects were evaluated by biventricular pressure-volume loop recordings, right heart catheterization, and arterial and mixed venous blood gasses. Pulmonary embolism increased mean pulmonary arterial pressure from 15 ± 4 to 33 ± 6 mm Hg (p = 0.0002) and caused right ventricle dysfunction (p < 0.05) with troponin release (p < 0.0001). In group 1, increasing FIO2 lowered mean pulmonary arterial pressure (p < 0.0001) and pulmonary vascular resistance (p = 0.0056) and decreased right ventricle volumes (p = 0.0018) and right ventricle mechanical work (p = 0.034). Oxygenation was improved and pulmonary shunt was lowered (p < 0.0001). Maximal hemodynamic effects were seen at FIO2 40% with no additional benefit from higher fractions of oxygen. In group 2, the effects of FIO2 40% were persistent over 75 minutes. Supplemental oxygen showed the same pulmonary vasodilator efficacy as inhaled nitric oxide (40 parts per million). No adverse effects were observed. CONCLUSIONS: In a porcine model mimicking intermediate-high risk pulmonary embolism, oxygen therapy reduced right ventricle afterload and lowered right ventricle mechanical work. The effects were immediately present and persistent and were similar to inhaled nitric oxide. The intervention is easy and safe. The study motivates extended clinical evaluation of supplemental oxygen in acute pulmonary embolism.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lyhne, Mads Dam","Hansen, Jacob Valentin","Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-227287"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Lyhne, Mads Dam"],"keyword":["Animal model","Inhaled nitric oxide","Pressure volume loops","Pulmonary circulation","Right ventricular function","Vasodilatation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"oxygen therapy lowers right ventricular afterload in experimental acute pulmonary embolism","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85107182056","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-227287","identifier":[{"id":"33870917","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85107182056","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000687033000010","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-abc294af-5c04-4868-9c7a-dd6daa4d7c8d"],"keyword":["animal model","inhaled nitric oxide","pressure-volume loops","pulmonary circulation","right ventricular function","vasodilatation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A04A94355D9BC39EFFE5A5FFFFB0953E"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Dam","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Valentin","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"31e33719-b4f4-428d-8342-33c42a4c78c2","name":"Hansen, Jacob Valentin","orcid":"0000-0002-7017-575X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Simone Juel","key":"dragsbækpau","last":"Dragsbæk","locid":"9c395385-9047-41cb-9a96-a1cdc538fdcf","name":"Dragsbaek, Simone Juel","name_org":"Dragsbæk, Simone Juel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Schmidt","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33870917"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1097/ccm.0000000000005057","issn":["00903493","10637389"],"issn_org":["00903493"],"issue":"9","journal":"Critical Care Medicine","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Critical Care Medicine","volume":"49"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85107182056"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-abc294af-5c04-4868-9c7a-dd6daa4d7c8d"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Oxygen Therapy Lowers Right Ventricular Afterload in Experimental Acute Pulmonary Embolism","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-227287","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aims: Left atrial appendage occlusion (LAAO) may be considered for patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation (NVAF) and a relative/formal contraindication to anticoagulation. This study aimed to summarize the impact of aging on LAAO outcomes at short and long-term follow-up. Methods and results: We compared subjects aged <70, ≥70 and <80, and ≥80 years old in the prospective, multicentre Amplatzer™ Amulet™ Occluder Observational Study (Abbott, Plymouth, MN, USA). Serious adverse events (SAEs) were reported from implant through a 2-year post-LAAO visit and adjudicated by an independent clinical events committee. Overall, 1088 subjects were prospectively enrolled. There were 265 subjects (24.4%) <70 years old, 491 subjects (45.1%) ≥70 and <80 years old, and 332 subjects (30.5%) ≥80 years old, with the majority (≥80%) being contraindicated to anticoagulation. As expected, CHA2DS2-VASc and HAS-BLED Scores increased with age. Implant success was high (≥98.5%) across all groups, and the proportion of subjects with a procedure- or device-related SAE was similar between groups. At follow-up, the observed ischaemic stroke rate was not significantly different between groups, and corresponding risk reductions were 62, 56, and 85% when compared with predicted rates for subjects <70, ≥70 and <80, and ≥80 years old, respectively. Major bleeding and mortality rates increased with age, while the incidence of device-related thrombus tended to increase with age. Conclusions: Despite the increased risk for ischaemic stroke with increasing age in AF patients, LAAO reduced the risk for ischaemic stroke compared with the predicted rate across all age groups without differences in procedural SAEs.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Freixa, Xavier","Schmidt, Boris","Mazzone, Patrizio","Berti, Sergio","Fischer, Sven","Lund, Juha","Montorfano, Matteo","Della Bella, Paolo","Lam, Simon Cheung Chi","Cruz-Gonzalez, Ignacio","Gage, Ryan","Omran, Heyder","Tarantini, Giuseppe","Aminian, Adel","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-263124"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Freixa, Xavier"],"country":["Belgium","Finland","Germany","Hong Kong","Italy","Spain","United States"],"glorg":["Abbott Laboratories","All Global Affiliations","CHU de Charleroi","Cardioangiologisches Centrum Bethanien","Gabriele Monasterio Foundation","Harzklinikum Dorothea Christiane Erxleben GmbH","Hospital Clinic de Barcelona","Pancreas Translational and Clinical Research Center","St. Marien Hospital","Universidad de Salamanca","University of Hong Kong","University of Padova","University of Turku"],"keyword":["Atrial fibrillation","Bleeding","Left atrial appendage occlusion","Outcomes","Stroke"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparative data on left atrial appendage occlusion efficacy and clinical outcomes by age group in the amplatzer amulet occluder observational study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85102088647","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-263124","identifier":[{"id":"33279979","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85102088647","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ea6ac279-6e81-4304-b3b7-99c909259021"],"keyword":["Atrial fibrillation","Bleeding","Left atrial appendage occlusion","Outcomes","Stroke"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C7B911A4A6BC23F0A12C260F5080F211"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"hospital clinic de barcelona","loc":["Spain",