8ef01-f8ed-4c49-a126-2109e2fd351f","name":"Holmvang, Lene","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisette O","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"b78c452e-5a69-41fe-ba49-0096c84d1d4d","name":"Jensen, Lisette Okkels","name_org":"Jensen, Lisette O","orcid":"0000-0002-4838-2429","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"kjaergaardpau","last":"Kjaergaard","name":"Kjaergaard, Jesper","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1 2","first":"Christian","key":"hassagerpau","last":"Hassager","locid":"49196e5d-0f61-44b2-a6b9-cd6c5f8f3bc2","name":"Hassager, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-1199-0981","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31961195"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2048872619896063","issn":["20488726","20488734"],"issn_org":["20488734"],"issue":"6","journal":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","pages":"557-566","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","title_org":"European Heart Journal - Acute Cardiovascular Care","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088297874"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3d94576a-00de-4d1d-9e1f-bd8a0f402440","sdu-dd3f2fa1-3137-4e30-8f3a-767f19701ccc"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Biomarkers predictive of late cardiogenic shock development in patients with suspected ST-elevation myocardial infarction","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.45-041706","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: To compare the hemodynamic effects of increased versus decreased preload in a porcine model of acute intermediate-risk pulmonary embolism. Design: Randomized, controlled animal study. Setting: Tertiary medical center, animal research laboratory. Subjects: Female, Danish slaughter pigs (n = 22, ∼ 60 kg). Interventions: Acute pulmonary embolism was induced by large emboli made from clotting of autologous blood. Sixteen animals were randomized to either fluid loading (n = 8, isotonic saline, 1 L/hr for 2 hr) or diuretic treatment (n = 8, furosemide, 40 mg every 30 min, total 160 mg) and compared with a vehicle group (n = 6, no treatment). Measurements and Main Results: Hemodynamics were evaluated at baseline, after pulmonary embolism and after each dose by biventricular pressure-volume loops, invasive pressures, diuretic output, respiratory variables, and blood analysis. Pulmonary embolism increased mean pulmonary arterial pressure (p < 0.0001), pulmonary vascular resistance (p = 0.008), right ventricular arterial elastance (p = 0.003), and right ventricular end-systolic volume (p = 0.020) while right ventricular stroke volume and right ventricular ejection fraction were decreased (p = 0.047 and p = 0.0003, respectively) compared with baseline. Fluid loading increased right ventricular end-diastolic volume (+31 ± 13 mL; p = 0.004), right ventricular stroke volume (+23 ± 10 mL; p = 0.009), cardiac output (+2,021 ± 956 mL; p = 0.002), and right ventricular ejection fraction (+7.6% ± 1.5%; p = 0.032), whereas pulmonary vascular resistance decreased (-202 ± 65 dynes; p = 0.020) compared with vehicle. Diuretic treatment decreased right ventricular end-diastolic volume (-84 ± 11 mL; p < 0.001), right ventricular stroke volume (-40 ± 6 mL; p = 0.001), cardiac output (-3,327 ± 451 mL; p = 0.005), and mean pulmonary arterial pressure (-7 ± 1 mm Hg; p < 0.001) and increased right ventricular end-systolic elastance (+0.72 ± 0.2 mm Hg/mL; p < 0.001) and systemic vascular resistance (+1,812 ± 767 dynes; p < 0.001) with no effects on mean arterial pressure. Conclusions: In a porcine model of acute intermediate-risk pulmonary embolism, fluid loading increased right ventricular preload and right ventricular stroke volume, whereas diuretics decreased right ventricular preload and right ventricular stroke volume without affecting mean arterial pressure.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","Kramer, Anders","Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","Lyhne, Mads Dam","Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","Andersen, Asger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-157166"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animal model","Diuretics","Fluid Therapy","Preload","Right ventricular function"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"impact of preload on right ventricular hemodynamics in acute pulmonary embolism","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85097003168","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-157166","identifier":[{"id":"33060507","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85097003168","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-30b3afb1-f298-4153-b269-d36af4eadabd"],"keyword":["animal model","diuretics","fluid therapy","preload","right ventricular function"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-ECC3A2F6E24360515689A97460B40122"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian S","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"e206a965-77bd-46fd-b3d4-5cd99f2d6b7c","name":"Mortensen, Christian Schmidt","name_org":"Mortensen, Christian S","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"kramerpau","last":"Kramer","locid":"be3123b5-5f55-4c8a-930c-f0f39cbc2981","name":"Kramer, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-5546-8693","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob","key":"schultzpau","last":"Schultz","locid":"9825d55c-97ee-4bab-a003-801dcd6d6df8","name":"Schultz, Jacob Gammelgaard","name_org":"Schultz, Jacob","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads D","key":"lyhnepau","last":"Lyhne","locid":"2bc2ac75-93d9-4c4c-b3cc-c6e80f5c7325","name":"Lyhne, Mads Dam","name_org":"Lyhne, Mads D","orcid":"0000-0001-5279-260X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Erik","key":"nielsenkudskpau","last":"Nielsen-Kudsk","locid":"8629b763-e23e-41d9-ac4f-61579d19565e","name":"Nielsen-Kudsk, Jens Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Asger","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a87aec57-562e-4950-b4cf-6741aae86cf1","name":"Andersen, Asger","orcid":"0000-0002-9102-3130","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33060507"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1097/ccm.0000000000004643","issn":["00903493","10637389"],"issn_org":["00903493"],"issue":"12","journal":"Critical Care Medicine","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Critical Care Medicine","volume":"48"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097003168"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-30b3afb1-f298-4153-b269-d36af4eadabd"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Impact of Preload on Right Ventricular Hemodynamics in Acute Pulmonary Embolism","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-157166","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"IT projects form an essential part of the ongoing transition towards increased digitalization in the public sector. However, according to the extant literature, IT projects in both the public and private sector frequently experience cost and schedule overruns, and some fail altogether. In contrast to the private sector, the scientific knowledge on IT projects in the public sector is limited in several ways. To contribute to this knowledge gap, we present the results of an empirical analysis of 48 completed central government IT projects. Our study analyses data on projects completed from 2011 – 2018, which have been submitted to The Danish Council for ICT following established guidelines and a mandated IT project model. Our study offers empirical and methodological contributions. We present up-to-date valid empirical data about cost and schedule overruns in government IT projects at the national level submitted to the Danish Council for ICT for risk assessment. We find that these IT projects, on average, experience much lower cost overruns than those reported in previous studies. Moreover, we find that these projects, on average, do experience schedule overruns, but lower than those reported in previous studies. At the methodological level, we demonstrate how sampling bias can be addressed in an analysis of IT projects through the application of the Heckman correction method. This sampling bias is an acknowledged, but unaddressed issue in previous project management research.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Krancher, Oliver","Schmidt, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383674"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Schedule overrun"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cost and schedule overrun in government it projects an empirical analysis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383674","ids":["itu-a4c5b9a6-4ca5-4cc7-92ac-86c897ddefae"],"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Schedule overrun"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E48340428CF04F879B5162CF7C3CA5A2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"e0edf6ca-5a14-41d8-81cc-77fd67f03ee5","name":"Schmidt, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-2500-0467","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a4c5b9a6-4ca5-4cc7-92ac-86c897ddefae"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cost and schedule overrun in Government IT projects. : An empirical analysis","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383674","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Background: 20-40% of patients with inflammatory arthritis (IA) do not tolerate or do not have sufficient effect of the medication and experience considerable problems in their everyday lives. For these patients multidisciplinary rehabilitation and coherence in healthcare are of utmost importance (1). However, only little is known about coherence in healthcare among patients with inflammatory arthritis Objectives: To explore how persons with IA experience coherence in their rehabilitation pathways Methods: Semi-structured individual interviews with 11 persons with IA who had experience with rehabilitation across primary and secondary health care. Interviews were conducted in the patients’ home before admission to an inpatient rehabilitation stay, during the admission and 2-3 weeks and 4-6 months after discharge. In addition, participant observations and informal interviews were performed during admission. The analysis involved case descriptions (2) and interpretation in a thematic analysis (3). Results: The analysis derived a main theme “a person centered approach”, representing the importance for the person to meet professionals who listen and acknowledge the persons own views rather than focusing on the “system”, regulations and standards. In addition, 4 sub-themes were identified: 1)“empowerment and disempowerment”, covering how most patients want to be in control and take action but they may lack the energy and ability to express their needs and thus give up; 2) “Lack of communication and coordination”, involving processes between the staff in the same department, between departments or sectors. Patients feel forced to take on coordinating tasks themselves, which they do not feel qualified to perform; 3) “Interventions meant as help may be felt as restrictions”, which encompass i.e. free physical therapy delivered at times not appropriate for the patient and types of support which can lead to a feeling of social control, and finally, 4) “The system is difficult to get through”. Information about possible support are provided at random and some ask for a coordinating person. Conclusion: Facilitators for coherent pathways among people with IA encompass dedicated professionals working with a person-centered approach aiming to empower people. This encompass to provide relevant knowledge and enable the person to ask for the right type of help. A coordinator may facilitate coherence.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Feddersen, Helle","Søndergaard, Jens","Schmidt, B. M.","Andersen, L.","Primdahl, Jette"],"cuid":["S02-095.45-698346"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Chronically ill","Communication","Coordination","Integrated care","Person centred care"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-OTH"],"OT":["Patient Forskningspartner"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCS"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-OTH","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"coherent rehabilitation experiences of people with inflammatory arthritis","src":["sdu","ucsyd"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCS"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1136/annrheumdis-2020-eular.4451","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-095.45-698346","ids":["sdu-c52f8c41-9eae-4854-ae46-fb802af359af","ucsyd-0ff2c238-76ec-4006-a4af-d94af1d0e81f"],"keyword":["chronically ill","communication","coordination","integrated care","person-centred care"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-034038B5C16D804E69213ED3D0332AC7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1136/annrheumdis-2020-eular.4451","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu 