{"abstract":"Aims/hypothesisThis study aimed to compare the increase in plasma glucose after a subcutaneous injection of 200g glucagon given after 45min of cycling with resting (study 1) and to investigate the effects of glucagon when injected before compared with after 45min of cycling (study 2). We hypothesised that: (1) the glucose response to glucagon would be similar after cycling and resting; and (2) giving glucagon before the activity would prevent the exercise-induced fall in blood glucose during exercise and for 2h afterwards.MethodsFourteen insulin-pump-treated individuals with type 1 diabetes completed three visits in a randomised, placebo-controlled, participant-blinded crossover study. They were allocated by sealed envelopes. Baseline values were (mean and range): HbA(1c) 54mmol/mol (43-65mmol/mol) or 7.1% (6.1-8.1%); age 45years (23-66years); BMI 26kg/m(2) (21-30kg/m(2)); and diabetes duration 26years (8-51years). At each visit, participants consumed a standardised breakfast 2h prior to 45min of cycling or resting. A subcutaneous injection of 200g glucagon was given before or after cycling or after resting. The glucose response to glucagon was compared after cycling vs resting (study 1) and before vs after cycling (study 2).ResultsThe glucose response to glucagon was higher after cycling compared with after resting (mean SD incremental peak: 2.61.7 vs 1.82.0mmol/l, p=0.02). As expected, plasma glucose decreased during cycling (-3.1 +/- 2.8mmol/l) but less so when glucagon was given before cycling (-0.9 +/- 2.8mmol/l, p=0.002). The number of individuals reaching glucose values 3.9mmol/l was the same on the 3days.Conclusions/interpretationModerate cycling for 45min did not impair the glucose response to glucagon compared with the glucose response after resting. The glucose fall during cycling was diminished by a pre-exercise injection of 200g glucagon; however, no significant difference was seen in the number of events of hypoglycaemia.Trial registrationThe study was funded by the Danish Diabetes Academy founded by Novo Nordisk foundation and by an unrestricted grant from Zealand Pharma","author_count":6,"contributors":["Steineck, Isabelle I. K.","Ranjan, Ajenthen","Schmidt, Signe","Clausen, Trine R.","Holst, Jens Juul","Nørgaard, Kirsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-521469"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Exercise","Glucagon","Type 1 diabetes"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"preserved glucose response to low dose glucagon after exercise in insulin pump treated individuals with type 000000000001 diabetes a randomised crossover study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-521469","identifier":[{"id":"000460556900004","type":"wos"},{"id":"30643924","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85060096116","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-d645b076-dbd3-47fb-839f-8af41f1ea672"],"keyword":["Exercise","Glucagon","Insulin pump-treated","Type 1 diabetes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-6-E3C87B7AEAEA3CEC2F863339ADF2FDF5"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Endocrinology and Metabolism","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Juul Holst Group","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, NNF Center for Basic Metabolic Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Isabelle I. K.","key":"steineckpau","last":"Steineck","name":"Steineck, Isabelle I. K.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ajenthen","key":"ranjanpau","last":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Ajenthen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Signe","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Signe","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Trine R.","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","name":"Clausen, Trine R.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Juul","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kirsten","key":"norgaardpau","last":"Norgaard","locid":"771b216e-eeb8-449e-8cde-2cdd59ae6dbc","name":"Nørgaard, Kirsten","name_org":"Norgaard, Kirsten","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30643924"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00125-018-4807-8","issn":["0012186X","14320428"],"issn_org":["0012186X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Diabetologia","pages":"582-592","status":"p","title":"Diabetologia","volume":"62"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85060096116"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d645b076-dbd3-47fb-839f-8af41f1ea672"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Preserved glucose response to low-dose glucagon after exercise in insulin-pump-treated individuals with type 1 diabetes : a randomised crossover study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-521469","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The creation of novel strategies, the pursuit of entrepreneurial opportunities, and the development of new technologies, capabilities, products, or business models all involve solving complex problems that require making a large number of highly interdependent choices. The challenge that complex problems pose to boundedly rational managers—the need to find a high-performing combination of interdependent choices—is akin to identifying a high peak on a rugged performance “landscape” that managers must discover through sequential search. Building on the NK model that Levinthal introduced into the management literature in 1997, scholars have used simulation methods to construct performance landscapes and examine various aspects of effective search processes. We review this literature to identify common themes and mechanisms that may be relevant in different managerial contexts. Based on a systematic analysis of 71 simulation studies published in leading management journals since 1997, we identify six themes: learning modes, problem decomposition, cognitive representations, temporal dynamics, distributed search, and search under competition. We explain the mechanisms behind the results and map all of the simulation articles to the themes. In addition, we provide an overview of relevant empirical studies and discuss how empirical and formal work can be fruitfully combined. Our review is of particular relevance for scholars in strategy, entrepreneurship, or innovation who conduct empirical research and apply a process lens. More broadly, we argue that important insights can be gained by linking the notion of search in rugged performance landscapes to practitioner-oriented practices and frameworks, such as lean startup or design thinking.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Baumann, Oliver","Schmidt, Jens","Stieglitz, Nils"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-578372"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Schmidt, Jens"],"country":["Finland","Germany"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","Frankfurt School of Finance & Management"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effective search in rugged performance landscapes a review and outlook","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/159907836/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-578372","identifier":[{"id":"85058663673","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-122c3833-aebc-4ade-bc07-5823ab93bf8c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B5CF245B4BB7A10B5F2E33127002052F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/159907836/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Business & Management (DBM)","SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Business & Management (DBM), Strategic Organization Design (SOD)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"frankfurt school of finance management","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Frankfurt School of Finance & Management","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Oliver","key":"baumannpau","last":"Baumann","locid":"fffff073-7696-455d-95ab-e86c33419e5f","name":"Baumann, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0002-0797-9464","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nils","key":"stieglitzpau","last":"Stieglitz","name":"Stieglitz, Nils","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/0149206318808594","issn":["01492063","15571211"],"issn_org":["01492063"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Management","pages":"285-318","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"p","title":"Journal of Management","volume":"45"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85058663673"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-122c3833-aebc-4ade-bc07-5823ab93bf8c"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Effective Search in Rugged Performance Landscapes : A Review and Outlook","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-578372","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The present manuscript highlights the economic profit increase when combining organic waste anaerobic digestion with other mixed culture anaerobic fermentation technologies, e.g., lactic acid fermentation and dark fermentation. Here we consider the conversion of 50 t/day of food waste into methane, power generation (from CHP of biomethane), lactic acid, polylactic acid, hydrogen, acetic acid, and butyric acid. The economic assessment shows that the basic alternative, i.e., anaerobic digestion with methane selling to the grid, generates 19 USD/t_VS (3 USD/t_foodwaste) of profit. The highest profit is obtained by dark fermentation with separation and purification of acetic and butyric acids, i.e., 296 USD/t_VS (47 USD/t_foodwaste). The only alternative that presented losses is the power generation alternative, needing tipping fees and/or subsidy of 176 USD/t_VS (29 USD/t_foodwaste). The rest of the alternatives generate profit. From the return on investment (ROI) and payback time, the best scenario is the production of polylactic acid, with 98% ROI and 7.8 years payback time. Production of butyric acid ROI and payback time was 74% and 9.1 years.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Bastidas Oyanedel, Juan-Rodrigo","Schmidt, Jens Ejbye"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-641949"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East"],"corresponding_author":["Bastidas Oyanedel, Juan-Rodrigo"],"country":["United Arab Emirates"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Khalifa University"],"keyword":["Anaerobic digestion","Biorefinery","Butyric acid","Dark fermentation","Food waste","Lactic acid fermentation","Polylactic acid"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"techno economic analysis of fermentation based biorefinery creating value from food residues","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85077374099","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-641949","identifier":[{"id":"85077374099","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-f00ed6d0-10e7-4cb8-85e9-697b64e11a9d"],"keyword":["Anaerobic digestion","Biorefinery","Butyric acid","Dark fermentation","Food waste","Lactic acid fermentation","Polylactic acid"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6597B6D409B9DCF765A551519D8C69CA"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ae","key":"khalifa university","loc":["United Arab Emirates","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Khalifa University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Green Technology (IGT)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan-Rodrigo","key":"bastidasoyanedelpau","last":"Bastidas Oyanedel","name":"Bastidas Oyanedel, Juan-Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Ejbye","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"14ca7f5b-030e-46a8-8aef-a09f6fa018f5","name":"Schmidt, Jens Ejbye","orcid":"0000-0001-6067-5032","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Juan-Rodrigo","key":"bastidasoyanedelpedh","last":"Bastidas-Oyanedel","name":"Bastidas-Oyanedel, Juan-Rodrigo","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jens Ejbye","key":"schmidtpedh","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Jens Ejbye","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-0