,"rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Caroline Schmidt","key":"laugesenpau","last":"Laugesen","name":"Laugesen, Caroline Schmidt","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8 2 4 9","corresponding":null,"first":"Katja","key":"schielkepau","last":"Schielke","locid":"060067d2-7693-4ac9-9977-d4a06bfe24a9","name":"Schielke, Katja Christina","name_org":"Schielke, Katja","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"skovpau","last":"Skov","name":"Skov, Jesper","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 10 4 11","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Toke","key":"bekpau","last":"Bek","locid":"ffc6d181-f10a-40f1-9446-0a5680b91ab8","name":"Bek, Toke","orcid":"0000-0002-0409-2534","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30311394"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aos.13936","issn":["1755375X","17553768"],"issn_org":["1755375X"],"issue":"8","journal":"Acta Ophthalmologica","pages":"763-769","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Acta Ophthalmologica","volume":"96"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054855212"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Other Research","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","dansk oftalmologisk selskab","organization of danish ophthalmologists"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-f4350202-bb68-4df1-a8d8-60e04d11d195","au-8233361c-9707-4475-9ca8-c283df9e213c","ku-5bda1499-9e4c-410e-9537-64c8fcbef149","sdu-3f0eac3e-a3dc-49a7-b813-29c3644d8405"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Evidence-based Danish guidelines for screening of diabetic retinopathy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-100.00-011677","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"One major source of new space debris are spacecraft (S/C) that are not removed from orbit after the end of their operational lifetime. Many regulations (e.g. ISO 24113) require the removal of S/C at the end of operation - known as Post-Mission-Disposal (PMD) - with a compliance rate of 90% to ensure that S/C do not become a new source of space debris. An analysis performed by ESA shows that the success rate of PMD in 2013 was in the range of about 50%-60%. The goal of TeSeR (Technology for Self-Removal) is to take the first step towards the development of a cost-efficient, but highly reliable PMD module. This PMD module is to be attached to the S/C on ground and it shall ensure the PMD of the S/C at the end of the operational lifetime. This PMD module shall be scalable and flexible, thus, enabling the PMD of any future S/C in an Earth orbit. Ultimately, the gap between the required 90% PMD success rate and the current success rate can be closed. The technological enhancements and developments required for successful PMD are addressed and analysed in TeSeR. The project's primary aims are to develop, manufacture and test an on-ground prototype of the PMD module, to develop three different removal subsystems (solid propulsion, electro-dynamical systems and deployable structures) for easy plug-in/plug-out implementation to the PMD module. This is the first step to demonstrate the main aspects of such a PMD module and the required main technologies. The technical activities are supported by non-technical tasks, e.g. investigation of legal issues relating to a PMD module, execution of a market study and consideration of this technology as a leverage to advance ISO norms. This double tracked approach ensures that the technological developments are embedded into the needs of the space community right from the start. Up to now the prototypes of the three removal subsystems have been developed, manufactured and tested with a common interface for implementation into the PMD module prototype. The PMD module prototype will be manufactured until summer 2018. Afterwards the removal subsystems will be integrated via the same interface. Airbus is the coordinator (and potential launch customer) of TeSeR. The project is conducted together with 10 notable institutes and companies from all across Europe with experts who have been working in the space debris issue for many years.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Voigt, Philipp","Vogt, Cornelius","Schubert, Ralf","Stokes, Hedley","Underwood, Craig","Cornogolub, Alexandru","Macdonald, Malcolm","Kerr, Emma","Smith, Lesley Jane","FÃ¶rstner, Roger","Wander, Alexandra","Konstantinidis, Kostas","Valli, Monica","Brilli, Simone","Lips, Tobias","PÃ©rez, Laura LeÃ³n","Ghizoni, Leonardo","Kristensen, Anders Schmidt","Nielsen, Jens Frederik Dalsgaard","McInnes, Colin","Bensoussan, Denis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-100309"],"event":{"end":"2018-10-05","place":"Bremen, de","start":"2018-10-01","title":"69th International Astronautical Congress: #InvolvingEveryone, IAC 2018","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Voigt, Philipp"],"country":["Germany","Italy","Netherlands","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Airbus Group","All Global Affiliations","Beazley Group","D-Orbit SpA","HTG Hyperschall Technologie GÃ¶ttingen GmbH","PHS Space Ltd","University of Glasgow","University of Strathclyde","University of Surrey","Universität der Bundeswehr München","Weber-Steinhaus and Smith"],"keyword":["Autonomous","Modular","Post Mission Disposal","Prototype","Space Debris","Standardized"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"teser technology for self removal status of a horizon 000000002020 project to ensure the post mission disposal of any future spacecraft","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85065343134&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 687295."],"id":"S01-000.00-100309","identifier":[{"id":"85065343134","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-ad2bf849-fc8a-4797-9c19-9343b7b3d96a"],"keyword":["Autonomous","Modular","Post-mission-disposal","Prototype","Space debris","Standardized"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-A2B29CB47243683ED51637DA0C007FBC"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nl","key":"airbus group","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Airbus Group","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"phs space ltd","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"PHS Space Ltd","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of surrey","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Surrey","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of strathclyde","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Strathclyde","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"weber steinhaus and smith","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Weber-Steinhaus and Smith","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"de","key":"universität der bundeswehr münchen","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universität der Bundeswehr München","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"it","key":"d orbit spa","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"D-Orbit SpA","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"de","key":"htg hyperschall