{"abstract":"Aim: Climate change results in increasing temperature and modified precipitation regimes in the High Arctic. Models can help anticipate the consequences on future biotic dynamics, e.g. vegetation. In rugged terrain, forecasts should consider fine-scale spatial variability in environmental conditions that are proximally linked to plant performance. Here, we forecasted Arctic plant community response to future climate change using high-resolution environmental variables. Location: Zackenberg in the High Arctic of Greenland. Methods: Using a combination of remote-sensing data and field measurements, we interpolated soil moisture and temperature at 1 m resolution together with spatial data on snow cover and solar radiation. We calibrated stacked species distribution models (S-SDMs) with data from 200 vegetation plots. To explore the sensitivity of Arctic communities to climate change, we forecasted these models under simulated increases in temperature and changes (positive or negative) in snow cover and soil moisture, corresponding to more winter and/or summer precipitation and higher frequencies of summer droughts. Results: S-SDMs associated with high-resolution environmental variables were able to reproduce the spatial variation in species richness and plant community structure along a mountain slope in Zackenberg. Model forecasts under climate change revealed that most species reacted to a combination of changes in soil moisture and temperature, and changes in these two variables resulted in an extensive restructuring of the distributions of species assemblages. In most scenarios, a gradual homogenization of communities was forecasted due to shrub expansion. Main conclusions: Increasing temperatures and altered soil moisture were predicted to turn the currently highly heterogeneous tundra landscape at Zackenberg into homogenous dwarf-shrub tundra. Such homogenization of vegetation communities may have profound ramifications for species, interaction webs, and ecosystem processes via modifications to the surface albedo, energy and water balance, as well as snow accumulation and permafrost.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Stewart, Lærke","Simonsen, Caroline Ernberg","Svenning, Jens Christian","Schmidt, Niels Martin","Pellissier, Loïc"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-169714"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Lausanne"],"keyword":["Climate change","Plant communities","Soil moisture","Species distribution modelling","Vegetation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"forecasted homogenisation of high arctic vegetation communities under climate change","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85054600075","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-169714","identifier":[{"id":"85054600075","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-46d068d8-2e57-4b9c-b5f3-dfc7c0c855b3"],"keyword":["climate change","model forecasts","plant communities","soil moisture","species distribution modelling","vegetation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-88AEA1FBA8E62DE823E8CE1779556C7A"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Biology, Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity","Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Ecoscience, Department of Ecoscience - Arctic Ecosystem Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of lausanne","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lausanne","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lærke","key":"stewartpau","last":"Stewart","locid":"ed422d15-911b-42ba-9d48-d14831ad84f9","name":"Stewart, Lærke","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Caroline Ernberg","key":"simonsenpau","last":"Simonsen","locid":"187d2b18-9e25-4e1e-a0e6-888af3361178","name":"Simonsen, Caroline Ernberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens-Christian","key":"svenningpau","last":"Svenning","locid":"33c3c4e2-57ab-478c-889d-06c594e57b8b","name":"Svenning, Jens Christian","name_org":"Svenning, Jens-Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-3415-0862","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels Martin","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"b91704ca-e126-4c35-af20-95b2269d230a","name":"Schmidt, Niels Martin","orcid":"0000-0002-4166-6218","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Loïc","key":"pellissierpau","last":"Pellissier","name":"Pellissier, Loïc","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/jbi.13434","issn":["03050270","13652699"],"issn_org":["03050270"],"issue":"11","journal":"Journal of Biogeography","pages":"2576-2587","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Publishing Ltd","status":"p","title":"Journal of Biogeography","volume":"45"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054600075"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-46d068d8-2e57-4b9c-b5f3-dfc7c0c855b3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Forecasted homogenisation of high-arctic vegetation communities under climate change","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-169714","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Essentials The risk of thrombosis among ischemic stroke patients using glucocorticoids is unknown. We examined the risk of thrombosis in 98 487 ischemic stroke patients, by glucocorticoid use. Myocardial infarction and venous thromboembolism risk was increased in glucocorticoid users. Hemorrhagic stroke risk was lower and recurrent ischemic stroke the same in glucocorticoid users. Summary: Background Glucocorticoid users have a high mortality rate following stroke, but the underlying clinical pathways are poorly understood. Objectives To examine the risk of cardiovascular events among ischemic stroke patients using glucocorticoids. Methods We conducted a nationwide population-based cohort study by using medical registries in Denmark. We identified all patients hospitalized with a first-time ischemic stroke (2004–2013). We categorized glucocorticoid use into current use (last prescription redemption ≤ 90 days before admission), former use, and non-use. With non-users as reference, we studied the risks of recurrent ischemic stroke, hemorrhagic stroke, myocardial infarction and venous thromboembolism associated with glucocorticoid use. Comorbidity and comedication-adjusted 1-year hazard ratios (aHRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were computed on the basis of Cox regression analysis. Results We identified 98 487 patients with a first-time (index) ischemic stroke. After the index stroke, the 1-year cumulative incidence of recurrent ischemic stroke was 16.4% among current glucocorticoid users, whereas risks were lower for hemorrhagic stroke (0.46%), myocardial infarction (1.35%), and venous thromboembolism (0.98%). Among current glucocorticoid users, aHRs were increased for myocardial infarction (1.32, 95% CI 0.98–1.76) and venous thromboembolism (1.39, 95% CI 0.99–1.94), whereas the risk of hemorrhagic stroke was reduced (aHR 0.60, 95% CI 0.38–0.93). There was no association with recurrent ischemic stroke (aHR 1.01, 95% CI 0.94–1.09). Conclusions During the first year after ischemic stroke, current glucocorticoid use was associated with moderately increased risks of myocardial infarction and venous thromboembolism, and a lower risk of hemorrhagic stroke, whereas the risk of recurrent ischemic stroke was not affected.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Sundbøll, Jens","Darvalics, Bianka","Horváth-Puhó, Erzsébet","Adelborg, Kasper","Laugesen, Kristina","Schmidt, Morten","Henderson, Victor W","Sørensen, Henrik Toft"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-174605"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cohort study","Glucocorticoids","Myocardial Infarction","Stroke","Venous thromboembolism"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"preadmission use of glucocorticoids and risk of cardiovascular events in patients with ischemic stroke","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85053843797","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-174605","identifier":[{"id":"85053843797","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30179297","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4f7d7a72-b2c8-49e9-bc77-8c245dd96d10"],"keyword":["cohort study","glucocorticoids","myocardial infarction","stroke","venous thromboembolism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3B34825AA06956C2DF5850AC1627A518"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"sundbøllpau","last":"Sundbøll","locid":"47396c7b-4f59-4aef-9cb1-0c63a6a8bbe2","name":"Sundbøll, Jens","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bianka","key":"darvalicspau","last":"Darvalics","locid":"add1939e-ca22-4035-af0f-ad88240332ea","name":"Darvalics, Bianka","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Erzsébet","key":"horváthpuhópau","last":"Horváth-Puhó","locid":"20f5c6bd-6ac1-4edb-a393-f34d60db546c","name":"Horváth-Puhó, Erzsébet","orcid":"0000-0002-3594-2212","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"adelborgpau","last":"Adelborg","locid":"2744cdbf-8516-46f6-ad71-f99fee1afaa0","name":"Adelborg, Kasper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristina","key":"laugesenpau","last":"Laugesen","locid":"ab62af11-21a5-4b44-a8ec-6c6d2afc742d","name":"Laugesen, Kristina","orcid":"0000-0001-7971-8223","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"21e3a3e6-e25e-48cf-b243-b8f43dca3bb8","name":"Schmidt, Morten","orcid":"0000-0002-4935-4059","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Victor","key":"hendersonpau","last":"Henderson","locid":"a6c3676e-d457-47df-8395-54e559a6c4c6","name":"Henderson, Victor W","name_org":"Henderson, Victor","orcid":"0000-0003-1198-9240","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Toft","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"4d49d93a-4913-4223-ba59-272e717a845d","name":"Sørensen, Henrik Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-4299-7040","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30179297"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/jth.14283","issn":["15387836","15387933"],"issn_org":["15387933"],"issue":"11","journal":"Journal of Thrombosis and Haemostasis","pages":"2175-83","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"Journal of Thrombosis and Haemostasis","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85053843797"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4f7d7a72-b2c8-49e9-bc77-8c245dd96d10"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Preadmission use of glucocorticoids and risk of cardiovascular events in patients with ischemic stroke","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-174605","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Nomenclatural type definitions are one of the most important concepts in biological nomenclature. Being physical objects that can be re-studied by other researchers, types permanently link taxonomy (an artificial agreement to classify biological diversity) with nomenclature (an artificial agreement to name biological diversity). Two proposals to amend the International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants (ICN), allowing DNA sequences alone (of any region and extent) to serve as types of taxon names for voucherless fungi (mainly putative taxa from environmental DNA sequences), have been submitted to be voted on at the 11th International Mycological Congress (Puerto Rico, July 2018). We consider various genetic processes affecting the distribution of alleles among taxa and find that alleles may not consistently and uniquely represent the species within which they are contained. Should the proposals be accepted, the meaning of nomenclatural types would change in a fundamental way from physical objects as sources of data to the data themselves. Such changes are conducive to irreproducible science, the potential typification on artefactual data, and massive creation of names with low information content, ultimately causing nomenclatural instability and unnecessary work for future researchers that would stall future explorations of fungal diversity. We conclude that the acceptance of DNA sequences alone as types of names of taxa, under the terms used in the current proposals, is unnecessary and would not solve the problem of naming putative taxa known only from DNA sequences in a scientifically defensible way. As an alternative, we highlight the use of formulas for naming putative taxa (candidate taxa) that do not require any modification of the ICN.","author_count":412,"contributors":["Zamora, Juan Carlos","Svensson, Mans","Kirschner, Roland","Olariaga, Ibai","Ryman, Svengunnar","Alberto Parra, Luis","Geml, Jozsef","Rosling, Anna","Adamcik, Sla