4191279ab","name":"Christensen, Johnny","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim Christian","key":"houlindpau","last":"Houlind","locid":"405b9fac-bba4-46ff-8d96-f40eeee21d12","name":"Houlind, Kim Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-1600-4415","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-b182808b-d3ef-42ba-a7b1-c49b2237b7e4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Calcified in-stent restenosis in a venous stent","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-624067","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"KEY POINTS: The power-asymptote (critical power; CP) of the hyperbolic power-time relationship for high-intensity exercise defines a threshold between steady-state and non-steady-state exercise intensities and the curvature constant (W') indicates a fixed capacity for work >CP that is related to a loss of muscular efficiency. The present study reports novel evidence on the muscle metabolic underpinnings of CP and W' during whole-body exercise and their relationships to muscle fibre type. We show that the W' is not correlated with muscle fibre type distribution and that it represents an elevated energy contribution from both oxidative and glycolytic/glycogenolytic metabolism. We show that there is a positive correlation between CP and highly oxidative type I muscle fibres and that muscle metabolic steady-state is attainable <CP but not >CP. Our findings indicate a mechanistic link between the bioenergetic characteristics of muscle fibre types and the power-time relationship for high-intensity exercise. ABSTRACT: We hypothesized that: (1) the critical power (CP) will represent a boundary separating steady-state from non-steady-state muscle metabolic responses during whole-body exercise and (2) that the CP and the curvature constant (W') of the power-time relationship for high-intensity exercise will be correlated with type I and type IIx muscle fibre distributions, respectively. Four men and four women performed a 3 min all-out cycling test for the estimation of CP and constant work rate (CWR) tests slightly >CP until exhaustion (Tlim ), slightly <CP for 24 min and until the >CP Tlim isotime to test the first hypothesis. Eleven men performed 3 min all-out tests and donated muscle biopsies to test the second hypothesis. Below CP, muscle [PCr] [42.6 ± 7.1 vs. 49.4 ± 6.9 mmol (kg d.w.)(-1) ], [La(-) ] [34.8 ± 12.6 vs. 35.5 ± 13.2 mmol (kg d.w.)(-1) ] and pH (7.11 ± 0.08 vs. 7.10 ± 0.11) remained stable between ∼12 and 24 min (P > 0.05 for all), whereas these variables changed with time >CP such that they were greater [[La(-) ] 95.6 ± 14.1 mmol (kg d.w.)(-1) ] and lower [[PCr] 24.2 ± 3.9 mmol (kg d.w.)(-1) ; pH 6.84 ± 0.06] (P < 0.05) at Tlim (740 ± 186 s) than during the <CP trial. The CP (234 ± 53 W) was correlated with muscle type I (r = 0.67, P = 0.025) and inversely correlated with muscle type IIx fibre proportion (r = -0.76, P = 0.01). There was no relationship between W' (19.4 ± 6.3 kJ) and muscle fibre type. These data indicate a mechanistic link between the bioenergetic characteristics of different muscle fibre types and the power-duration relationship. The CP reflects the bioenergetic characteristics of highly oxidative type I muscle fibres, such that a muscle metabolic steady-state is attainable below and not above CP.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Vanhatalo, Anni","Black, Matthew I","DiMenna, Fred J","Blackwell, Jamie R","Schmidt, Jakob Friis","Thompson, Chris","Wylie, Lee J","Mohr, Magni","Bangsbo, Jens","Krustrup, Peter","Jones, Andrew M"],"cuid":["S02-097.02-046362"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Columbia University","University of Exeter","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the mechanistic bases of the power time relationship muscle metabolic responses and relationships to muscle fibre type","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85027958414","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-097.02-046362","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4967754","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"26940850","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85027958414","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-d00a8b2c-2f82-4bd6-9b0c-94952dc3baaf","sdu-75854c41-4148-4b03-8474-f0241adfa59e"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C945A7C713E7741676E30C7F84DA3F68"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of exeter","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Exeter","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"columbia university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Columbia University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, The August Krogh Section for Human Physiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":5,"key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anni","key":"vanhatalopau","last":"Vanhatalo","name":"Vanhatalo, Anni","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthew I","key":"blackpau","last":"Black","name":"Black, Matthew I","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Fred J","key":"dimennapau","last":"DiMenna","name":"DiMenna, Fred J","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jamie R","key":"blackwellpau","last":"Blackwell","name":"Blackwell, Jamie R","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Jakob Friis","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"74b58d72-77a1-4d66-aaaa-c36189cbf0b6","name":"Schmidt, Jakob Friis","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Chris","key":"thompsonpau","last":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, Chris","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lee J","key":"wyliepau","last":"Wylie","name":"Wylie, Lee J","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Magni","key":"mohrpau","last":"Mohr","name":"Mohr, Magni","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Jens","key":"bangsbopau","last":"Bangsbo","locid":"24839560-f889-42a6-9b73-97b69f101548","name":"Bangsbo, Jens","orcid":"0000-0003-1305-9274","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Peter","key":"krustruppau","last":"Krustrup","locid":"9b334221-94f3-4a68-a2de-ee9f419704d8","name":"Krustrup, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9843-7320","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrew M","key":"jonespau","last":"Jones","name":"Jones, Andrew M","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26940850"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1113/jp271879","issn":["00223751","14697793"],"issn_org":["00223751"],"issue":"15","journal":"Journal of Physiology","pages":"4407-4423","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Journal of Physiology","title_org":"The Journal of Physiology","volume":"594"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85027958414"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d00a8b2c-2f82-4bd6-9b0c-94952dc3baaf","sdu-75854c41-4148-4b03-8474-f0241adfa59e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The mechanistic bases of the power-time relationship : Muscle metabolic responses and relationships to muscle fibre type","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.02-046362","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: A decline in physical capacity takes place with increasing age that negatively affects overall physical function including work ability and the ability to perform typical activities of daily living (ADL). The overall aim of the present study was to determine the neuromuscular adaptations to long-term (1 year) football and strength training in older untrained adults, and to assess the concurrent effect on functional ADL capacity. METHODS: Twenty-seven healthy elderly males (68.2 ± 3.2 years) were randomly assigned to 12 months of either recreational football training (FT: n = 10), strength training (ST: n = 9) or served as inactive controls (CON: n = 8). Recreational football training consisted of small-sided training sessions whereas strength training consisted of high intensity exercises targeting the lower extremity and upper body. Maximal thigh muscle strength and rate of force development (RFD) were assessed with isokinetic dynamometry, while postural balance and vertical jumping performance were evaluated using force plate analysis. Furthermore, functional ability was evaluated by stair-ascent and chair-rising testing. RESULTS: A total of nine, nine and seven participants from FT, ST and CON, respectively, were included in the analysis. Both exercise regimens led to substantial gains in functional ability, evidenced by 24 and 18 % reduced stair-ascent time, and 32 and 21 % increased chair-rising performance in FT and ST, respectively (all P < 0.05). Long-term strength training led to increased concentric (14 %; P < 0.01) and isometric (23 %; P < 0.001) quadriceps and isometric hamstring strength (44 %; P < 0.0001), whereas football training mainly resulted in enhanced hamstring strength (18 %, P < 0.05) and RFD (89 %, P < 0.0001). CONCLUSIONS: Long-term (1 year) strength training led to increased quadriceps and hamstring strength, whereas the adaptations to football training mainly included enhanced strength and rapid force capacity of the hamstring muscles. Gains in functional ability were observed in response to both training regimens, evidenced by reduced stair-ascent time and increased chair-rising performance. Long-term football exercise and strength training both appear to be effective interventional strategies to improve factors of importance for ADL by counteracting the age-related decline in lower limb strength and functional capacity among old male adults. This could potentially be a way to improve work ability of senior workers.","author_count":22,"contributors":["Sundstrup, Emil","Jakobsen, Markus Due","Andersen, Lars L.","Andersen, Thomas Rostgaard","Randers, Morten Bredsgaard","Helge, Jørn Wulff","Suetta, Charlotte","Schmidt, Jakob Friis","Bangsbo, Jens","Krustrup, Peter","Aagaard, Per"],"cuid":["S04-099.60-766768"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Exeter"],"keyword":["Activities of Daily Living","CMJ","Hamstring","Muscle Strength","Postural Balance","Quadriceps","RFD","Sit to stand"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"positive effects of 000000000001 year football and strength training on mechanical muscle function and functional capacity in elderly men","src":["aau","ku","nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84962808845","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S04-099.60-766768","identifier":[{"id":"2