{"abstract":"The aim of the study was to determine whether lifelong football training may improve cardiovascular function, physical fitness, and body composition. Our subjects were 17 male veteran football players (VPG; 68.1 ± 2.1 years) and 26 healthy age-matched untrained men who served as a control group (CG; 68.2 ± 3.2 years). Examinations included measurements of cardiac function, microvascular endothelial function [reactive hyperemic index (RHI)], maximum oxygen uptake (VO2max), and body composition. In VPG, left ventricular (LV) end-diastolic volume was 20% larger (P < 0.01) and LV ejection fraction was higher (P < 0.001). Tissue Doppler imaging revealed an augmented LV longitudinal displacement, i.e., LV shortening of 21% (P < 0.001) and longitudinal 2D strain was 12% higher (P < 0.05), in VPG. In VPG, resting heart rate was lower (6 bpm, P < 0.05), and VO2max was higher (18%, P < 0.05). In addition, RHI was 21% higher (P < 0.05) in VPG. VPG also had lower body mass index (P < 0.05), body fat percentage, total body fat mass, android fat percentage, and gynoid fat percentage (all P < 0.01). Lifelong participation in football training is associated with better LV systolic function, physical fitness, microvascular function, and a healthier body composition. Overall, VPG have better cardiovascular function compared with CG, which may reduce their cardiovascular morbidity and mortality.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Schmidt, Jakob Friis","Rostgaard Andersen, Thomas","Andersen, Lars Juel","Randers, Morten Bredsgaard","Hornstrup, Therese","Hansen, Peter Riis","Bangsbo, Jens","Krustrup, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-099.51-047447"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Exeter"],"keyword":["Adipose tissue","Aged","Body Mass Index","Body composition","Cardiovascular System","Case Control Studies","Echocardiography","Endurance training","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Oxygen Consumption","Peripheral arterial tonometry","Physical Fitness","Research Support Non U S Gov t","Soccer","Vasodilation","Ventricular Function Left"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cardiovascular function is better in veteran football players than age matched untrained elderly healthy men","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84921345537","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.51-047447","identifier":[{"id":"24303918","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84921345537","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-f633c862-905c-4d94-a63d-e376f7dc248e","sdu-09ddde16-a0e3-45c7-b3aa-0e8679454ee8"],"keyword":["Adipose Tissue","Aged","Body Composition","Body Mass Index","Cardiovascular System","Case-Control Studies","Echocardiography","Endurance training","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Oxygen Consumption","Peripheral arterial tonometry","Physical Fitness","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Soccer","Vasodilation","Ventricular Function, Left","Veteran athletes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-A06E220418718B33DB7DB5FB9991CECC"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of S