{"abstract":"Objective. To study the role of endogenous glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) on gastric emptying rates of a solid meal as well as postprandial hormone secretion and glucose disposal. Material and methods. In nine healthy subjects, gastric emptying of a 310-kcal radio-labelled solid meal and plasma concentrations of insulin, glucagon and glucose were measured during infusion of saline or the GLP-1 receptor antagonist exendin(9-39)amide (Ex(9-39)) at 300 pmol·kg-1·min-1. Results. Ex(9-39) infusion had no effect on the total gastric emptying curve, but changed the intra-gastric distribution of the meal. During infusion of Ex(9-39), more content stayed in the upper stomach (79.1 ± 2.5% of total during Ex(9-39) compared to 66.6 ± 5.7% during saline at 5 min). During Ex(9-39) infusion, higher concentrations of plasma glucagon were measured both before (after 40 min of Ex(9-39) infusion the glucagon level was 15.1 ± 0.7 pmol·L-1 compared to 5.4 ± 1.4 during saline) and after the meal, and postprandial GLP-1 levels increased. Basal insulin and glucose levels were not affected by Ex(9-39), but the postprandial rise of insulin and glucose enhanced during Ex(9-39). Conclusions. Endogenous GLP-1 is involved in the regulation of gastric motility in relation to meal intake and also in the regulation of postprandial insulin and glucose levels. Furthermore, endogenous GLP-1 seems to tonically restrain glucagon secretion.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Witte, Anne-Barbara","Grybäck, Per","Jacobsson, Hans","Näslund, Erik","Hellström, Per Martin","Holst, Jens Juul","Hilsted, Linda","Schmidt, Peter Thelin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-445705"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adult","Blood Glucose","Female","Gastric Emptying","Glucagon","Glucagon Like Peptide 1","Glucose","Humans","Hunger","Insulin","Male","Nausea","Pancreas","Peptide Fragments","Peptide YY","Postprandial Period","Satiation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"involvement of endogenous glucagon like peptide 000000000001 in regulation of gastric motility and pancreatic endocrine secretion","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3109/00365521.2010.537680","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-445705","identifier":[{"id":"21114428","type":"pmid"},{"id":"79952611982","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-576f3a6e-08cb-462e-bf81-aae78a25ee25"],"keyword":["Adult","Blood Glucose","Female","Gastric Emptying","Glucagon","Glucagon-Like Peptide 1","Glucose","Humans","Hunger","Insulin","Male","Nausea","Pancreas","Peptide Fragments","Peptide YY","Postprandial Period","Satiation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-8287AF6EE20E812E2BF7844C49DCDAF0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3109/00365521.2010.537680","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Section of Surgery and Internal Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anne-Barbara","key":"wittepau","last":"Witte","locid":"0568726e-d8f4-467d-b0f3-24e0c5b57601","name":"Witte, Anne-Barbara","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Per","key":"grybäckpau","last":"Grybäck","name":"Grybäck, Per","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hans","key":"jacobssonpau","last":"Jacobsson","name":"Jacobsson, Hans","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Erik","key":"näslundpau","last":"Näslund","name":"Näslund, Erik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Per Martin","key":"hellströmpau","last":"Hellström","name":"Hellström, Per Martin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Juul","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Linda","key":"hilstedpau","last":"Hilsted","locid":"b51cc15d-e5af-476e-b0bd-12b56481f0bd","name":"Hilsted, Linda","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Peter Thelin","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Peter Thelin","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["21114428"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3109/00365521.2010.537680","issn":["00365521","15027708"],"issn_org":["00855928"],"issue":"4","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Gastroenterology","pages":"428-35","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Gastroenterology","title_org":"Scandinavian Journal of Gastroenterology. Supplement","volume":"46"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79952611982"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-576f3a6e-08cb-462e-bf81-aae78a25ee25"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Involvement of endogenous glucagon-like peptide-1 in regulation of gastric motility and pancreatic endocrine secretion","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-445705","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"Context: Therapeutic proteins/peptides can produce immunogenic responses that may increase the risk of adverse events or reduce efficacy. Objective: The objectives were to measure and characterize antibody formation to liraglutide, a glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist, to investigate the impact on glycemic control and safety, and to compare it with exenatide, an agent in the same class. Design: Antibody data were collected during six Liraglutide Effect and Action in Diabetes (LEAD) trials (26–104 wk duration). Setting: Samples for determination of antibody formation were collected at LEAD trial sites and analyzed at central laboratories. Participants: Antibodies were measured in LEAD trial participants with type 2 diabetes. Interventions: Interventions included once-daily liraglutide (1.2 or 1.8 mg) or twice-daily exenatide (10 µg). Main Outcome Measures: The main outcome measures included the proportion of patients positive for anti-liraglutide or anti-exenatide antibodies, a glucagon-like peptide-1 cross-reacting effect, and an in vitro liraglutide- or exenatide-neutralizing effect. Change in glycosylated hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) by antibody status and magnitude [negative, positive (high or low level)]. Results: After 26 wk, 32 of 369 (8.7%) and 49 of 587 (8.3%) patients had low-level antibodies to liraglutide 1.2 and 1.8 mg, respectively [mean 3.3% antibody-bound radioactivity out of total radioactivity (%B/T), range 1.6–10.7%B/T], which did not attenuate glycemic efficacy (HbA1c reductions 1.1–1.3% in antibody-positive vs. 1.2% in antibody-negative patients). In LEAD-6, 113 of 185 extension patients (61%) had anti-exenatide antibodies at wk 26 (range 2.4–60.2%B/T). High levels of anti-exenatide antibodies were correlated with significantly smaller HbA1c reductions (P = 0.0022). After switching from exenatide to liraglutide, anti-exenatide antibodies did not compromise a further glycemic response to liraglutide (additional 0.4% HbA1c reduction). Conclusions: Liraglutide was less immunogenic than exenatide; the frequency and levels of anti-liraglutide antibodies were low and did not impact glycemic efficacy or safety.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Buse, John B","Garber, Alan","Rosenstock, Julio","Schmidt, Wolfgang E","Brett, Jason H","Videbæk, Nicoline","Holst, Jens Juul","Nauck, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-456523"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Antibody Formation","Blood Glucose","Diabetes Mellitus Type 2","Female","Glucagon Like Peptide 1","Humans","Male","Peptides","Radioimmunoassay","Venoms"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"liraglutide treatment is associated with a low frequency and magnitude of antibody formation with no apparent impact on glycemic response or increased frequency of adverse events results from the liraglutide effect and action in diabetes lead trials","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2010-2822","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-456523","identifier":[{"id":"21450987","type":"pmid"},{"id":"79956328908","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-699a827c-9cc4-4c3a-bd2a-9ce56c76641e"],"keyword":["Antibody Formation","Blood Glucose","Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2","Female","Glucagon-Like Peptide 1","Humans","Male","Peptides","Radioimmunoassay","Venoms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-8A88B0610D9A175CF8AF11671C7613B2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2010-2822","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, NNF Center for Basic Metabolic Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"John B","key":"busepau","last":"Buse","name":"Buse, John B","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Alan","key":"garberpau","last":"Garber","name":"Garber, Alan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Julio","key":"rosenstockpau","last":"Rosenstock","name":"Rosenstock, Julio","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Wolfgang E","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Wolfgang E","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jason H","key":"brettpau","last":"Brett","name":"Brett, Jason H","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Nicoline","key":"videbækpau","last":"Videbæk","name":"Videbæk, Nicoline","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Juul","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michael","key":"nauckpau","last":"Nauck","name":"Nauck, Michael","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["21450987"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1210/jc.2010-2822","issn":["0021972X","19457197"],"issn_org":["0021972X"],"issue":"6","journal":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","pages":"1695-702","status":"p","title":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","volume":"96"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79956328908"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-699a827c-9cc4-4c3a-bd2a-9ce56c76641e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Liraglutide Treatment Is Associated with a Low Fr