us":"p","title":"Food Additives & Contaminants: Part A - Chemistry, Analysis, Control, Exposure & Risk Assessment","title_org":"Food Additives & Contaminants: Part A","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79960334134"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Undetermined","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-35d540f1-63f9-4644-87ec-d1e8334baa90","ku-afce4fad-aec7-4ef2-979f-5e194fe11edd"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Migration of nanosized layered double hydroxide platelets from polylactide nanocomposite films","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-089.77-033787","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"An analytical platform coupling asymmetric flow field-flow fractionation (AF4) with multiangle light scattering (MALS), dynamic light scattering (DLS), and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICPMS) was established and used for separation and quantitative determination of size and mass concentration of nanoparticles (NPs) in aqueous suspension. Mixtures of three polystyrene (PS) NPs between 20 and 100 nm in diameter and mixtures of three gold (Au) NPs between 10 and 60 nm in diameter were separated by AF4. The geometric diameters of the separated PS NPs and the hydrodynamic diameters of the Au and PS NPs were determined online by MALS and DLS, respectively. The three separated Au NPs were quantified by ICPMS and recovered at 50−95% of the injected masses, which ranged between approximately 8−80 ng of each nanoparticle size. Au NPs adhering to the membrane in the separation channel was found to be a major cause for incomplete recoveries. The lower limit of detection (LOD) ranged between 0.02 ng Au and 0.4 ng Au, with increasing LOD by increasing nanoparticle diameter. The analytical platform was applied to characterization of Au NPs in livers of rats, which were dosed with 10 nm, 60 nm, or a mixture of 10 and 60 nm nanoparticles by intravenous injection. The homogenized livers were solubilized in tetramethylammonium hydroxide (TMAH), and the recovery of Au NPs from the livers amounted to 86−123% of their total Au content. In spite of successful stabilization with bovine serum albumin even in alkaline medium, separation of the Au NPs by AF4 was not possible due to association with undissolved remains of the alkali-treated liver tissues as demonstrated by electron microscopy images.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Schmidt, Bjørn","Löschner, Katrin","Hadrup, Niels","Mortensen, Alicja","Sloth, Jens Jørgen","Bender Koch, Christian","Larsen, Erik Huusfeldt"],"cuid":["S02-096.58-033286"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-UND"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-UND"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"quantitative characterization of gold nanoparticles by field flow fractionation coupled online with light scattering detection and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/5558149/9A35F929d01.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-096.58-033286","identifier":[{"id":"79953282335","type":"scopus"},{"id":"21355549","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-2008c064-a447-4635-843d-53b884494d40","ku-c59b5ac6-6711-4cf1-93c8-84c32ed1bfe7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E7D2D6D1035E788210BE2AD0EAA9902B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/5558149/9A35F929d01.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/ac102545e","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute","Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute, Division of Food Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Life Sciences, Department of Basic Sciences and Environment, Environmental Chemistry and Physics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"unclear universities","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-UND","l3nl":"Undetermined","l3nn":"Unclear Universities","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Unclear Universities","namevar":["Division of Toxicology and Risk Assessment"],"nora_code":"DI-U-UND","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"2efd5b97-c8d6-4fc4-9612-532a1e298599","name":"Schmidt, Bjørn","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Katrin","key":"löschnerpau","last":"Löschner","locid":"d7b22217-70e4-4c54-bd53-3f02c2d23a6f","name":"Löschner, Katrin","orcid":"0000-0003-1741-8406","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels","key":"hadruppau","last":"Hadrup","locid":"4384548f-118a-4c3c-aedf-f0d203046277","name":"Hadrup, Niels","orcid":"0000-0002-1188-445X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Alicja","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"2cbb8a58-bb77-4b14-9b9b-16408c39d2c4","name":"Mortensen, Alicja","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Jørgen","key":"slothpau","last":"Sloth","locid":"d7fb8bd1-2626-48fa-b49e-35d57b2fae5d","name":"Sloth, Jens Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0002-3636-8769","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Christian","key":"benderkochpau","last":"Bender Koch","locid":"bb2fd1fd-66d8-4e6a-abb1-932df9520ec8","name":"Bender Koch, Christian","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Erik Huusfeldt","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"9528940a-884f-4a63-b068-b739d7551006","name":"Larsen, Erik Huusfeldt","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["21355549"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1021/ac102545e","issn":["00032700","15204782"],"issn_org":["00032700"],"issue":"7","journal":"Analytical Chemistry","pages":"2461-2468","publisher":"American Chemical Society","status":"p","title":"Analytical Chemistry","volume":"83"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79953282335"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Undetermined","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-2008c064-a447-4635-843d-53b884494d40","ku-c59b5ac6-6711-4cf1-93c8-84c32ed1bfe7"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Quantitative Characterization of Gold Nanoparticles by Field-Flow Fractionation Coupled Online with Light Scattering Detection and Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.58-033286","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"Agro‐biofuels are expected to reduce the emissions of greenhouse gases because CO2 emitted during the combustion of the biofuels has recently been taken from the atmosphere by the energy crop. Thus, when replacing fossil fuels with biofuels we reduce the emission of fossil fuel‐derived CO2 into the atmosphere. However, cultivation of the soil results in emission of other greenhouse gasses, especially nitrous oxide (N2O). Agricultural activity is the dominant source of N2O, which is produced by microbes in the soil when the nitrogen availability is high, for instance following fertilization or incorporation of crop residues. In this study we relate measured field emissions of N2O to the reduction in fossil fuel‐derived CO2, which is obtained when energy crops are used for biofuel production. The analysis includes five organically managed crops (viz. maize, rye, rye‐vetch, vetch and grass‐clover) and three scenarios for conversion of biomass to biofuel. The scenarios are 1) bioethanol production, 2) biogas production and 3) co‐production of bioethanol and biogas, where the energy crops are first used for bioethanol fermentation and subsequently the residues from this process are utilized for biogas production. The net reduction in greenhouse gas emissions is calculated as the avoided fossil fuel‐derived CO2, where the N2O emission has been subtracted. This value does not include farm machinery CO2 emissions and fuel consumption during biofuel production. Thus, the actual net greenhouse gas reduction will be lower than indicated by our data. We obtained the greatest net reduction in greenhouse gas emissions by co‐production of bioethanol and biogas or by biogas alone produced from either fresh grass‐clover or whole crop maize. Here the net reduction corresponded to about 8 tons CO2 per hectare per year. The worst result was obtained for bioethanol produced from vetch straw where high N2O emissions outweighed the avoided fossil fuel‐derived CO2.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Carter, Mette Sustmann","Hauggaard-Nielsen, Henrik","Heiske, Stefan","Thomsen, Sune Tjalfe","Jensen, Morten","Schmidt, Jens Ejbye","Johansen, Anders","Ambus, Per"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-139371"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"greenhouse gas emissions from cultivation of energy crops may affect the sustainability of biofuels","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-139371","ids":["au-116ae3f4-ebdc-4c80-ba9c-07fb25827cea"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DA4DDCB677B4F3D2CC519F7BDA6368DE"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark (DTU)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Environmental Science, Department of Environmental Science - Environment